\subsection{Tmux-Scoped Runtime State}
\label{track:tmux-state-dir}

The tracking directory above holds \emph{persistent} data: time
entries and session state that must survive reboots.  Tmux-scoped
state---window labels, pane labels, suspend state, the last-selected
window---is different in kind: it describes windows and panes of a
running tmux server, and those referents die with the machine.  An
earlier design kept both kinds in the persistent tracking directory,
which meant tmux-scoped files \emph{outlived} the windows they
described.  After a reboot, a fresh server reuses the same socket
path and restarts window numbering at [[@0]], so a surviving state
file could resurrect a dead window's labels in a brand-new, never
tracked window the moment their [[@N]] identifiers coincided.

We instead store tmux-scoped state where tmux stores its own
sockets: on the temporary filesystem, in [[nytid-<uid>]] next to
tmux's [[tmux-<uid>]].  A reboot then clears the state in the same
stroke that kills the windows it describes, so state can never
outlive its referents across reboots---the resurrection hazard
disappears structurally instead of needing cleanup code.

Three design issues deserve spelling out, because each of the
obvious alternatives is subtly wrong.

\begin{description}
\item[Why not [[$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR]]?]  It looks like the canonical
  choice for per-user runtime state, but systemd removes it when the
  user's last login session ends, while a tmux server routinely
  outlives the login session that started it.  Our state files would
  vanish under still-living windows---exactly the
  all-windows-lose-tracking failure this design exists to prevent.
  tmux itself keeps its sockets in [[/tmp]] for the same reason, and
  we follow it.  We honor [[$TMPDIR]] via
  [[tempfile.gettempdir()]], as tmux honors [[$TMUX_TMPDIR]].
\item[Why does the key still need a generation?]  The temporary
  filesystem is only cleared by a \emph{reboot}.  A server killed
  and restarted without one ([[tmux kill-server]], a crash) leaves
  the state files in place while the new server restarts window
  numbering at [[@0]], so the per-instance generation in the server
  key remains what keeps a predecessor's files inert (see the
  keying discussion below).  The two mechanisms are complementary:
  the location bounds the files' lifetime by the machine's uptime,
  the key scopes them to one server instance within it.
\item[Aging of temporary files]  Some systems periodically delete
  temporary files that have not been touched for a week or two.  A
  tracked window that nobody switches to for that long could lose
  its state file.  We accept this: the hooks rewrite state on every
  window switch, so only long-idle windows are exposed, tmux lives
  with the same exposure for its sockets, and [[nytid track adopt]]
  re-tracks an affected window in one command
  (\cref{track:adopt}).
\end{description}

Because the temporary directory is world-writable, another user
could squat our path before we first create it.  Like tmux, we
create the directory mode [[0700]] and refuse one owned by somebody
else---failing loudly beats silently sharing state with a stranger.
The location can be overridden through configuration (for tests and
unusual setups); the default is derived from the machine and user and
recomputed on each call rather than written back, so this helper
shares [[get_tracking_storage]]'s read-only property above.

It shares the caching too.  The lookup below reads through [[default]]
(\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}), so the eleven call sites answer from
one process-lifetime read of the configuration file rather than each
re-reading it from disk.  That saving is worth more here than for the
tracking paths above: several of those call sites sit on the tmux
window-event hook path, so under the old lookup every window switch
paid for a fresh read---latency the user feels while merely moving
around tmux.

[[default]] takes its fallback as an ordinary argument, so handing it
the derived location would evaluate that location on every call---and
this fallback, unlike [[get_tracking_storage]]'s
[[pathlib.Path.home()]], is not a pure computation.  The first
[[tempfile.gettempdir()]] in a process searches the candidate
directories by \emph{creating, writing and unlinking} a file in each,
and raises [[FileNotFoundError]] if none of them works.  The user whose
temporary directory is unusable is precisely the user who sets
[[track.tmux_state_dir]], so deriving eagerly would fail before ever
reading the setting meant to rescue them.

We therefore ask [[default]] for [[None]] and derive the fallback
ourselves only when [[None]] is what comes back, keeping the probe on
the one path whose answer uses it.  This treats a configured
[[null]] as a missing key, which is harmless here because neither
names a directory.

<<constants>>=
TMUX_STATE_DIR_CONFIG = "track.tmux_state_dir"
<<configuration management>>=
def get_tmux_state_dir() -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return the directory holding tmux-scoped state files.

    Defaults to nytid-<uid> under the system temporary directory,
    creating it with mode 0700.  Raises PermissionError if the
    directory is owned by another user.
    """
    configured = default(TMUX_STATE_DIR_CONFIG, None)
    if configured is None:
        state_dir = (
            pathlib.Path(tempfile.gettempdir())
            / f"nytid-{os.getuid()}"
        )
    else:
        state_dir = pathlib.Path(configured)
    state_dir.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    if state_dir.stat().st_uid != os.getuid():
        raise PermissionError(
            f"{state_dir} is not owned by the current user"
        )
    return state_dir
@

The lock guarding tmux context state protects only files that live
here, so it lives here too.

<<configuration management>>=
def get_tmux_context_lock_file() -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return the lock file guarding tmux context state."""
    return get_tmux_state_dir() / "tmux_context.lock"
@

