\subsection{Keying State by Server Instance}
\label{track:server-key}

Tmux-scoped state files---window labels, pane labels, suspend state,
the last-selected window---embed a \emph{server key} in their
filenames.  The key must identify a server \emph{instance}, not merely
a server address.  Hostname and socket path alone are not enough:
a server killed and restarted without a reboot ([[tmux
kill-server]], a crash) listens on the very same socket path, and
its window numbering restarts at [[@0]].  Under an address-only
key, a brand-new window would collide with the dead server's state
file for the same [[@N]] identifier, and nytid would resurrect the
dead window's labels in a window that was never tracked.  The
runtime-state location (\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}) covers only
the reboot variant of this hazard; a server death that keeps the
machine up also fires no [[pane-exited]] hooks, so the hook-based
cleanup (\cref{track:detached-finalize}) never gets a chance to
remove the files either.

We therefore extend the key with a \emph{generation}: the server's
PID and start time.  The start time guards against a deterministic
boot sequence handing a new server the same PID again.

Crucially, both ingredients must come from the server itself, not
from [[$TMUX]].  An earlier design read the PID out of [[$TMUX]],
which looked free of charge but is subtly wrong: the PID field in
[[$TMUX]] is whatever PID the client that \emph{created} the session
observed.  The very first client records the PID of tmux's short-lived
pre-daemonization fork, while every later client sees the real
daemon PID---so two panes of the \emph{same live server} can
disagree about the PID, and with it about the key.  The observed
consequence was that windows launched from the original terminal and
windows launched from a later terminal were keyed as if they belonged
to two different servers, and each side treated the other's state as
stale.  Every tracked window then \enquote{lost} its tracked status
at once.  Asking the server for its own identity makes the key a
function of the server alone, so all clients agree.

The address part (the \emph{sockethash}) and the generation are
joined with hyphens---characters that [[sanitize_tmux_id]] passes
through unchanged---so every state filename carries the key as a
single underscore-free token from which the sockethash prefix is
recoverable, letting other code classify state files by the server
they belong to (\cref{track:server-sweep}).

<<configuration management>>=
def build_tmux_sockethash(
    hostname: str,
    socket_path: str,
) -> str:
    """Return the address part of a tmux server key."""
    digest = hashlib.sha256(
        f"{hostname}\0{socket_path}".encode()
    ).hexdigest()
    return digest[:16]

def build_tmux_server_key(
    hostname: str,
    socket_path: str,
    generation: str,
) -> str:
    """Return a stable key for one tmux server instance."""
    sockethash = build_tmux_sockethash(
        hostname, socket_path,
    )
    return f"{sockethash}-{generation}"
@

From [[$TMUX]], whose format is [[socket,server_pid,session_idx]],
we take only the socket path---the address.  We split from the right
because the socket path itself may contain commas.

<<configuration management>>=
def parse_tmux_socket_path(
    tmux_env: str | None,
) -> str | None:
    """Extract the tmux socket path from ``$TMUX`` if present."""
    if not tmux_env:
        return None
    try:
        socket_path, _server_pid, _session_idx = tmux_env.rsplit(",", 2)
    except ValueError:
        return tmux_env or None
    return socket_path or None
@

For the generation we ask the server, in a single query for both
facts, so they describe the same server instance.  One subprocess
call per CLI process is acceptable, but hook subprocesses and nested
commands may build several keys, so we memoize the answer per
[[$TMUX]] value.  A failed query yields [[None]] rather than an
exception: a server that cannot answer is dying or absent, and every
caller already treats a missing key as \enquote{no tmux context}.

<<configuration management>>=
def get_tmux_server_generation() -> str | None:
    """Return the tmux server's ``pid-start_time``, or ``None``."""
    tmux_env = os.environ.get("TMUX")
    if not tmux_env:
        return None
    if tmux_env in _TMUX_GENERATION_CACHE:
        return _TMUX_GENERATION_CACHE[tmux_env]
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "display-message",
                "-p",
                "#{pid}-#{start_time}",
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    generation = result.stdout.strip()
    if not generation.strip("-"):
        return None
    _TMUX_GENERATION_CACHE[tmux_env] = generation
    return generation
<<constants>>=
_TMUX_GENERATION_CACHE: dict[str, str] = {}
@

Assembling a key combines the address and the generation.  The
[[tmux_env]] parameter names the server whose namespace we are
keying, while the generation query always asks the ambient
server---the two agree because tmux client commands themselves
resolve the server from the process environment, which is also where
callers obtain the [[tmux_env]] they pass in.

<<configuration management>>=
def compute_tmux_server_key(
    tmux_env: str | None,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the instance key for the server named by ``tmux_env``."""
    socket_path = parse_tmux_socket_path(tmux_env)
    if not socket_path:
        return None
    generation = get_tmux_server_generation()
    if not generation:
        return None
    return build_tmux_server_key(
        socket.gethostname(),
        socket_path,
        generation,
    )
@

The everyday entry point adds one refinement: it returns [[None]] not
only outside tmux but also when this worker's filesystem holds no
state for the current socket at all.  All its callers use the key
solely to address \emph{existing} state (state files are only ever
created by [[run_tmux_command]], which computes its key directly via
[[compute_tmux_server_key]]), so when no state exists we can skip the
start-time query and keep untracked [[track start]]/[[stop]]
invocations free of tmux subprocess calls---the same hot-path
principle as [[has_tracked_window_state]].  The glob requires a
hyphen after the sockethash, so state written by the pre-generation
key format never reactivates this path.

<<configuration management>>=
def get_current_tmux_server_key() -> str | None:
    """Return the current tmux server instance key, or ``None``.

    Returns ``None`` outside tmux, and also when no tmux-scoped
    state exists for the current server's socket, so that callers
    addressing existing state stay subprocess-free.
    """
    tmux_env = os.environ.get("TMUX")
    socket_path = parse_tmux_socket_path(tmux_env)
    if not socket_path:
        return None
    sockethash = build_tmux_sockethash(
        socket.gethostname(), socket_path,
    )
    if not any(
        get_tmux_state_dir().glob(f"*_{sockethash}-*")
    ):
        return None
    return compute_tmux_server_key(tmux_env)
@

<<configuration management>>=
def get_current_session_file(
    who: str = None
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Get the path to the current session file for a worker."""
    if who is None:
        who = default_username
    tracking_dir = get_tracking_dir()
    suffix = sanitize_who(who)
    session_file = tracking_dir / f"current_session_{suffix}.json"

    # Backward compatibility: migrate old unsuffixed file
    if who == default_username and not session_file.exists():
        old_file = tracking_dir / "current_session.json"
        if old_file.exists():
            return old_file

    return session_file

def get_current_session_lock_file(
    who: str = None
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Get the lock file guarding one worker's active session."""
    session_file = get_current_session_file(who)
    return session_file.with_suffix(".lock")
@

