\section{Helper Functions}

We need some utility functions for formatting, display, and autocompletion.

\subsection{Label Display Constants}

When displaying labels in different contexts, we use consistent separators.
For CSV export (where labels appear in a single cell), we use a hierarchical
separator that clearly distinguishes label boundaries while remaining
human-readable.

<<constants>>=
# Label display separators
LABEL_SEPARATOR = " > "

# Hours expected per work day
DEFAULT_DAILY_LIMIT_HOURS = 8.0    # Standard work day
@

Durations are normally positive, but they must never be \emph{rendered}
as positive when they are not.  Python's [[divmod]] floors towards
negative infinity, so splitting a raw negative second count into hours,
minutes and seconds yields components that read as a plausible positive
duration: $-30$~minutes becomes [[30m 0s]], and so does $-90$~minutes.
A corrupt entry would then be indistinguishable from a healthy one in
[[status]], [[ls]], and [[stats]] output.

We therefore split the magnitude and carry the sign as an explicit
prefix.  This is a display safeguard rather than a licence to store
negative durations---the commands that build entries reject inverted
intervals---but a formatter that cannot lie is what makes such a bug
visible instead of silent.

Seconds are optional.  A single session is measured to the second, and
[[status]] and [[ls]] show it that way.  The period reports of
\cref{sec:track-stats} are sums over days or weeks, where
[[148h 20m 0s]] says nothing that [[148h 20m]] does not, and an
expected [[8h 0m 0s]] reads as a measurement rather than the target it
is.  The switch lives on this one formatter instead of in a second
function so that the two spellings cannot drift apart in the one place
where drifting would matter: the treatment of the sign.  A magnitude
that rounds to no whole minute is unsigned, since [[-0m]] would claim a
direction the number cannot show.

<<helper functions>>=
def format_duration(
    duration: datetime.timedelta,
    seconds: bool = True,
) -> str:
    """Format a duration in a human-readable way.

    Negative durations keep a leading minus sign so an
    inverted interval can never be displayed as a
    positive one.  With `seconds=False` the seconds are
    dropped: `1h 30m`, `30m`, `0m`.
    """
    total_seconds = int(duration.total_seconds())
    sign = "-" if total_seconds < 0 else ""
    hours, remainder = divmod(abs(total_seconds), 3600)
    minutes, secs = divmod(remainder, 60)

    if not seconds:
        if hours > 0:
            return f"{sign}{hours}h {minutes}m"
        elif minutes > 0:
            return f"{sign}{minutes}m"
        else:
            return "0m"
    if hours > 0:
        return f"{sign}{hours}h {minutes}m {secs}s"
    elif minutes > 0:
        return f"{sign}{minutes}m {secs}s"
    else:
        return f"{sign}{secs}s"

def get_labels_display(labels: list[str]) -> str:
    """Get a display string for labels (as comma-separated tags)"""
    return ", ".join(sorted(labels)) if labels else "No labels"
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_duration_is_sign_aware():
  """Negative durations must not render as positive ones."""
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=30)) == "30m 0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30)) == "-30m 0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(minutes=-90)) == "-1h 30m 0s"

def test_format_duration_without_seconds():
  """The minute-resolution form drops seconds but never the sign."""
  assert format_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(hours=148, minutes=20, seconds=5),
    seconds=False,
  ) == "148h 20m"
  assert format_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(hours=8), seconds=False
  ) == "8h 0m"
  assert format_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(minutes=-30), seconds=False
  ) == "-30m"
  assert format_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(seconds=-30), seconds=False
  ) == "0m"
@

A deviation is read in both directions: [[1h 30m]] beside an expected
[[8h 0m]] does not say whether the day ran over or under.
[[format_duration]] already refuses to hide a minus; this wrapper makes
the plus equally explicit.  Zero stays unsigned, for the same reason
[[-0m]] is never produced: a sign asserts a direction, and zero has
none.

<<helper functions>>=
def format_signed_duration(
    duration: datetime.timedelta,
    seconds: bool = True,
) -> str:
    """Format a deviation with an explicit `+` or `-` sign.

    Zero is unsigned; `seconds` is passed on to `format_duration`.
    """
    text = format_duration(duration, seconds=seconds)
    if text.startswith("-") or text in ("0s", "0m"):
        return text
    return "+" + text
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_signed_duration():
  """Deviations carry a sign in both directions, except zero."""
  assert format_signed_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(minutes=90), seconds=False
  ) == "+1h 30m"
  assert format_signed_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(hours=-2), seconds=False
  ) == "-2h 0m"
  assert format_signed_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(), seconds=False
  ) == "0m"
  assert format_signed_duration(
    datetime.timedelta(seconds=45)
  ) == "+45s"
  assert format_signed_duration(datetime.timedelta()) == "0s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta(seconds=-5)) == "-5s"
  assert format_duration(datetime.timedelta()) == "0s"
@

\subsubsection{Abbreviating Content Hashes}

The [[track ls --id]] view names each entry by a Git-style prefix of its
SHA256 [[content_hash()]].  We want the shortest prefix that still uniquely
identifies every displayed entry, so we start at a minimum length and widen
only when two prefixes collide.

The minimum matters more than it first appears.  A pure
shortest-unique-prefix rule would collapse to a \emph{single character}
whenever the first hex digits already differ---which is the common case with
only a handful of entries, and especially right after an edit changes the set
of hashes.  A one-character id is unreadable and feels unstable: it changes
length as entries come and go.  We therefore floor the prefix at six
characters, matching Git's default abbreviation, which is long enough to read
and re-type while the collision loop below still widens further on the rare
occasions six is not enough.

<<constants>>=
TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH = 6
@

Because [[resolve_tracking_entry]] matches any prefix with [[startswith]],
widening the displayed id never invalidates a shorter prefix a user may have
already copied---display length and lookup length are independent.

Widening only terminates if the inputs are pairwise distinct.  Two
distinct strings must differ at some position, so a wide enough prefix
eventually separates them; a repeated string, by contrast, can never be
separated from itself, and the loop below would widen past the length of
a SHA256 digest and keep going---an unresponsive [[track ls]] with no
output and no error.  The condition it tests, that the number of
\emph{distinct} prefixes has reached the number of inputs, simply cannot
be met when the inputs themselves are fewer than they are counted.

We therefore make distinctness the function's own precondition by
collapsing repeats on entry, rather than rejecting them.  The argument
for collapsing is that a content hash \emph{is} an identity: two entries
whose hashes agree agree in every field [[content_hash()]] digests, so
they are the same entry recorded twice, and there is no second id for
this function to invent.  The answer is a mapping keyed by hash, which
already has one slot per distinct hash, so dropping the repeat removes
nothing a caller could have observed.  Raising instead would push a
normalization concern back onto callers---and [[load_tracking_data]]
already merges exact duplicates before anyone gets here, which is why
the hang has stayed out of reach so far.  That is a property of today's
callers, though, not of this function, and the next caller need not
share it.

<<helper functions>>=
def abbreviate_hashes(full_hashes: list[str]) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Return unique short prefixes for full content hashes.

    Repeated hashes name the same content, so they collapse
    into one entry sharing one prefix.
    """
    distinct_hashes = list(dict.fromkeys(full_hashes))
    if not distinct_hashes:
        return {}

    prefix_length = TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
    while True:
        prefixes = {
            full_hash: full_hash[:prefix_length]
            for full_hash in distinct_hashes
        }
        if len(set(prefixes.values())) == len(distinct_hashes):
            return prefixes
        prefix_length += 1
@

Let's verify that a duplicated hash terminates rather than hangs.  The
first pair separates at the minimum length, so it checks the plain case;
the second pair collides at six characters and only separates at seven,
so it checks that collapsing repeats has not disturbed the widening the
loop exists to do.  A regression here does not fail an assertion---it
never returns---so the test hanging \emph{is} the failure signal.

<<test functions>>=
def test_abbreviate_hashes_tolerates_duplicates():
    """A repeated hash must not widen the prefix forever."""
    first = "a" * 64
    second = "b" * 64
    result = abbreviate_hashes([first, second, first])
    assert len(result) == 2
    assert result[first] == first[
        :TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
    ]
    assert result[second] == second[
        :TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
    ]

    colliding = abbreviate_hashes(
        ["abcdef0", "abcdef1", "abcdef0"]
    )
    assert colliding == {
        "abcdef0": "abcdef0",
        "abcdef1": "abcdef1",
    }
@

\subsubsection{Merging Overlapping Intervals}

Naively summing [[entry.duration()]] across all entries double-counts
wall-clock time whenever labels overlap---for instance, two labels
tracked from 09:00 to 10:00 would report two hours instead of one.
We avoid this by merging overlapping intervals before summing.  The
algorithm sorts intervals by start time, then greedily extends the
current merged interval as long as the next one overlaps; whenever
there is a gap it flushes the accumulated interval and starts a new
one.  We expose the interval-merging step directly because the stats
command needs it in two places: once for overall day totals, and once
for exact label combinations within a day.

<<helper functions>>=
def merge_interval_pairs(intervals) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Compute wall-clock time covered by ``(start, end)`` pairs."""
    intervals = sorted(intervals)
    if not intervals:
        return datetime.timedelta()

    merged_total = datetime.timedelta()
    cur_start, cur_end = intervals[0]

    for start, end in intervals[1:]:
        if start <= cur_end:
            cur_end = max(cur_end, end)
        else:
            merged_total += cur_end - cur_start
            cur_start, cur_end = start, end

    merged_total += cur_end - cur_start
    return merged_total

def merge_intervals(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Compute wall-clock time covered by entries.

    Overlapping intervals are merged so that concurrent
    labels do not inflate the total.
    """
    return merge_interval_pairs(
        (e.start_time, e.end_time) for e in entries
    )
@

\subsubsection{Daily Aggregation}

The stats report works per calendar date.  An entry that spans midnight
must be split across the two dates; otherwise all its time would land on
the start date.  We therefore split each entry at midnight boundaries
first.

There are two useful views of the split data:
\begin{description}
\item[Daily wall-clock totals] Merge all intervals within a day so
  overlapping labels do not inflate the day's total.
\item[Daily label-set totals] Keep exact label combinations together so
  repeated sessions with the same labels collapse into one line, while
  still merging overlaps within that label set.
\end{description}

The reports generate their date ranges explicitly
(\cref{sec:track-calendar-periods}) so that days off remain visible as
[[0s]] rather than disappearing.

<<helper functions>>=
def compute_daily_wall_clock(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> dict:
    """Compute wall-clock time per calendar date.

    Returns a dict mapping ``datetime.date`` to
    ``datetime.timedelta``, with overlapping intervals
    merged within each date.
    """
    date_intervals = {}
    for entry in entries:
        start = entry.start_time
        end = entry.end_time
        while start.date() < end.date():
            midnight = datetime.datetime.combine(
                start.date() + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
                datetime.time.min,
            )
            date_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), []
            ).append((start, midnight))
            start = midnight
        if start < end:
            date_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), []
            ).append((start, end))

    daily_totals = {}
    for date, intervals in date_intervals.items():
        daily_totals[date] = merge_interval_pairs(intervals)

    return daily_totals

def compute_daily_label_set_totals(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
) -> dict[datetime.date, dict[tuple, datetime.timedelta]]:
    """Compute merged time per date and exact label set."""
    date_label_intervals = {}
    for entry in entries:
        labels_key = tuple(sorted(entry.labels))
        start = entry.start_time
        end = entry.end_time
        while start.date() < end.date():
            midnight = datetime.datetime.combine(
                start.date() + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
                datetime.time.min,
            )
            date_label_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), {}
            ).setdefault(labels_key, []).append((start, midnight))
            start = midnight
        if start < end:
            date_label_intervals.setdefault(
                start.date(), {}
            ).setdefault(labels_key, []).append((start, end))

    daily_totals = {}
    for date, label_intervals in date_label_intervals.items():
        daily_totals[date] = {
            labels_key: merge_interval_pairs(intervals)
            for labels_key, intervals in label_intervals.items()
        }

    return daily_totals
@

\subsubsection{Calendar Periods}
\label{sec:track-calendar-periods}

The daily views above answer for one date at a time.  The period
reports of \cref{sec:track-stats} ask the same question over a week, a
month or a year, and then compare the answer with what the work week
(\cref{cli.utils.workweek}) says it should have been.  Four zoom
levels, one question: the only thing that varies is where the period
boundaries fall.  So the granularity becomes a value and there is one
implementation.  A [[str]]-backed [[Enum]] is the module's usual
spelling for such a closed choice, cf.\ [[ExportFormat]].

<<constants>>=
class StatsPeriod(str, Enum):
    daily = "daily"
    weekly = "weekly"
    monthly = "monthly"
    yearly = "yearly"
@

A period is the calendar unit containing a date, named by its
inclusive end points.  Weeks are \textsc{iso} weeks, Monday to
Sunday: that is the week a printed calendar shows, and it is what makes
\enquote{this week} a Monday-anchored question rather than a rolling
seven days.  Months and years are the calendar ones;
[[calendar.monthrange]] knows how long February is in a given year, so
no date library is needed.

<<helper functions>>=
def period_bounds(
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
    date: datetime.date,
) -> tuple[datetime.date, datetime.date]:
    """Return the inclusive (start, end) of the period holding `date`."""
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.weekly:
        start = date - datetime.timedelta(days=date.weekday())
        return start, start + datetime.timedelta(days=6)
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.monthly:
        last = calendar.monthrange(date.year, date.month)[1]
        return date.replace(day=1), date.replace(day=last)
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.yearly:
        return (
            date.replace(month=1, day=1),
            date.replace(month=12, day=31),
        )
    return date, date
@

Let's verify the bounds in a leap February, so that the month case is
checked where a fixed month length would go wrong.  15 February 2024
is a Thursday, whose week starts on Monday the 12th.

<<test functions>>=
def test_period_bounds():
  """Each granularity names the calendar unit around a date."""
  day = datetime.date(2024, 2, 15)
  assert period_bounds(StatsPeriod.daily, day) == (day, day)
  assert period_bounds(StatsPeriod.weekly, day) == (
    datetime.date(2024, 2, 12), datetime.date(2024, 2, 18)
  )
  assert period_bounds(StatsPeriod.monthly, day) == (
    datetime.date(2024, 2, 1), datetime.date(2024, 2, 29)
  )
  assert period_bounds(StatsPeriod.yearly, day) == (
    datetime.date(2024, 1, 1), datetime.date(2024, 12, 31)
  )
@

The label names the period the way a reader would: an \textsc{iso}
date, an \textsc{iso} week such as [[2026-W36]], a month such as
[[2026-09]], or a year.  The week label is computed from the period's
Monday, not from whichever date the report was asked about, and that
is what makes it right at year boundaries.  1 January 2027 is a Friday
in week 53 of 2026; asking its Monday, 28 December 2026, for its
\textsc{iso} year and week gives exactly [[2026-W53]].  A weekly
label's year can therefore differ from the calendar year of the dates
it covers.  That is a property of \textsc{iso} weeks, not a bug.

<<helper functions>>=
def period_label(
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
    start: datetime.date,
) -> str:
    """Name the period that starts on `start`."""
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.weekly:
        iso_year, iso_week, _ = start.isocalendar()
        return f"{iso_year}-W{iso_week:02d}"
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.monthly:
        return f"{start.year}-{start.month:02d}"
    if granularity == StatsPeriod.yearly:
        return str(start.year)
    return start.isoformat()
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_period_label_at_year_boundaries():
  """Weeks are labelled by their Monday's ISO year and week."""
  monday, _ = period_bounds(
    StatsPeriod.weekly, datetime.date(2027, 1, 1)
  )
  assert monday == datetime.date(2026, 12, 28)
  assert period_label(StatsPeriod.weekly, monday) == "2026-W53"

  monday, _ = period_bounds(
    StatsPeriod.weekly, datetime.date(2026, 1, 1)
  )
  assert period_label(StatsPeriod.weekly, monday) == "2026-W01"

  assert period_label(
    StatsPeriod.monthly, datetime.date(2026, 9, 1)
  ) == "2026-09"
  assert period_label(
    StatsPeriod.yearly, datetime.date(2026, 1, 1)
  ) == "2026"
  assert period_label(
    StatsPeriod.daily, datetime.date(2026, 9, 3)
  ) == "2026-09-03"
@

A report covers the most recent [[count]] periods, newest first, and
the newest is the one containing today even though it is not over
yet: a Wednesday report that hid the current week would be answering
last week's question.  The three values travel together as a named
tuple so that a caller can say [[period.label]] instead of remembering
a position.

<<helper functions>>=
class Period(NamedTuple):
    """A calendar period: inclusive end points and a display label."""

    start: datetime.date
    end: datetime.date
    label: str
@

Walking backwards needs no month arithmetic.  The day before a
period's start lies in the previous period whatever the
granularity---the day before 1 January is 31 December of the year
before---so [[period_bounds]] of that day \emph{is} the previous
period.  The function takes [[now]] so that tests can pin the
calendar and a command can capture the clock once and hand the same
instant to every helper.

<<helper functions>>=
def make_period_ranges(
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
    count: int,
    now: datetime.datetime | None = None,
) -> list[Period]:
    """Return the `count` most recent periods, newest first.

    The first period is the one containing today.
    """
    if now is None:
        now = datetime.datetime.now()

    periods = []
    start, end = period_bounds(granularity, now.date())
    for _ in range(count):
        periods.append(
            Period(start, end, period_label(granularity, start))
        )
        start, end = period_bounds(
            granularity, start - datetime.timedelta(days=1)
        )
    return periods
@

Let's verify the walk at the awkward places: across the year boundary
for weeks (4 January 2027 is a Monday), across February for months,
and across a month boundary for days.  Whatever the granularity,
consecutive periods must abut exactly.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_period_ranges_walks_backwards():
  """Periods come newest first and abut exactly."""
  now = datetime.datetime(2027, 1, 4, 9, 0)
  weeks = make_period_ranges(StatsPeriod.weekly, 2, now=now)
  assert [p.label for p in weeks] == ["2027-W01", "2026-W53"]
  assert weeks[0].start == datetime.date(2027, 1, 4)
  assert weeks[1] == Period(
    datetime.date(2026, 12, 28), datetime.date(2027, 1, 3), "2026-W53"
  )

  months = make_period_ranges(
    StatsPeriod.monthly, 3, now=datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 15)
  )
  assert [p.label for p in months] == ["2026-03", "2026-02", "2026-01"]
  assert months[1].end == datetime.date(2026, 2, 28)

  years = make_period_ranges(
    StatsPeriod.yearly, 2, now=datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 3)
  )
  assert [p.label for p in years] == ["2026", "2025"]

  days = make_period_ranges(
    StatsPeriod.daily, 3, now=datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 1)
  )
  assert [p.label for p in days] == [
    "2026-03-01", "2026-02-28", "2026-02-27"
  ]

  for granularity in StatsPeriod:
    periods = make_period_ranges(granularity, 3, now=now)
    for newer, older in zip(periods, periods[1:]):
      assert older.end + datetime.timedelta(days=1) == newer.start
@

A report's window is the union of its periods, and the entries must
be cut to it before anything is summed.  The cut is calendar-based,
not a rolling number of hours.  Rolling would mean that a report for
\enquote{today} shows the last 24 hours, which can exclude
early-morning work from today while including late-evening work from
yesterday.  Cutting at midnight instead keeps the part of an overnight
session that belongs to the window and drops the part that does not,
so the totals agree with the dates the report names.

<<helper functions>>=
def clip_entries_to_dates(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
    start_date: datetime.date,
    end_date: datetime.date,
) -> "list[TrackingEntry]":
    """Return entries clipped to the inclusive calendar date range.

    Entries entirely outside the range are dropped; an entry that
    crosses a boundary of the range is cut at midnight so that only
    the part inside remains.
    """
    window_start = datetime.datetime.combine(
        start_date,
        datetime.time.min,
    )
    window_end = datetime.datetime.combine(
        end_date + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
        datetime.time.min,
    )

    clipped = []
    for entry in entries:
        if entry.end_time <= window_start:
            continue
        if entry.start_time >= window_end:
            continue

        clipped.append(
            TrackingEntry(
                start_time=max(entry.start_time, window_start),
                end_time=min(entry.end_time, window_end),
                labels=entry.labels,
                description=entry.description,
                who=entry.who,
            )
        )

    return clipped
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_clip_entries_to_dates_uses_calendar_days():
    """Clipping uses calendar dates, not rolling 24-hour cutoffs."""
    entries = [
        TrackingEntry(
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 11, 23, 30, 0),
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 1, 0, 0),
            ["overnight"],
        ),
        TrackingEntry(
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 11, 8, 0, 0),
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 11, 9, 0, 0),
            ["yesterday"],
        ),
        TrackingEntry(
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 10, 0, 0),
            datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 11, 0, 0),
            ["today"],
        ),
    ]

    today = datetime.date(2026, 3, 12)
    clipped = clip_entries_to_dates(entries, today, today)

    assert len(clipped) == 2
    assert clipped[0].labels == ["overnight"]
    assert clipped[0].start_time == datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 0, 0, 0)
    assert clipped[0].end_time == datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 1, 0, 0)
    assert clipped[1].labels == ["today"]
@

Bucketing works on the daily totals, not on the entries.  The pipeline
is: clip to the window, split and merge per day with
[[compute_daily_wall_clock]], then add each day's total into the
period that contains it.  Every step is exact.  Splitting at midnight
puts an overnight session's hours on the two dates they belong to;
merging per day means two labels tracked side by side count once; and
the per-day totals can simply be added, because after the split no
interval crosses midnight, so two different days cannot overlap.
Bucketing entries directly would have to redo the merge per period
and would still get the overnight case wrong.

<<helper functions>>=
def totals_per_period(
    daily_totals: dict[datetime.date, datetime.timedelta],
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
) -> dict[datetime.date, datetime.timedelta]:
    """Sum per-date totals into periods, keyed by period start."""
    totals = {}
    for date, duration in daily_totals.items():
        start, _ = period_bounds(granularity, date)
        totals[start] = (
            totals.get(start, datetime.timedelta()) + duration
        )
    return totals
@

Let's verify the two properties the pipeline is built for: an
overnight session from Sunday 6 September 2026 into the Monday lands
in two different weeks, and two labels tracked over the same hour
count as one hour.

<<test functions>>=
def test_totals_per_period_sums_split_days():
  """An overnight entry is split between the weeks it spans."""
  entries = [
    TrackingEntry(
      datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 6, 23, 30),
      datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 7, 1, 0),
      ["overnight"],
    ),
  ]
  totals = totals_per_period(
    compute_daily_wall_clock(entries), StatsPeriod.weekly
  )
  assert totals == {
    datetime.date(2026, 8, 31): datetime.timedelta(minutes=30),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7): datetime.timedelta(hours=1),
  }

def test_totals_per_period_merges_overlapping_labels():
  """Concurrent labels do not inflate a period's total."""
  start = datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 7, 9, 0)
  end = datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 7, 10, 0)
  entries = [
