@

The expectation is the work week's answer to \enquote{how much should
have been tracked}: one daily limit per work day in the range.  The
caller clips the range to today, so the current week expects the work
days that have happened rather than all five.  A Wednesday report
would otherwise show a two-day deficit that no amount of work could
have closed.  Weekends are simply not work days, so any time tracked on
one is a deviation in the other direction, which is the point.

<<helper functions>>=
def expected_time(
    start_date: datetime.date,
    end_date: datetime.date,
    daily_limit: float,
    work_days: list[str] | None = None,
) -> datetime.timedelta:
    """Return `daily_limit` hours per work day in the inclusive range."""
    work_day_count = count_work_days(start_date, end_date, work_days)
    return datetime.timedelta(hours=work_day_count * daily_limit)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_expected_time_counts_only_workdays():
  """Only work days contribute to the expectation."""
  weekdays = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"]
  monday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 7)
  wednesday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 9)
  saturday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 12)
  sunday = datetime.date(2026, 9, 13)
  assert expected_time(monday, sunday, 8.0, weekdays) == (
    datetime.timedelta(hours=40)
  )
  assert expected_time(monday, wednesday, 8.0, weekdays) == (
    datetime.timedelta(hours=24)
  )
  assert expected_time(saturday, sunday, 8.0, weekdays) == (
    datetime.timedelta()
  )
@

\subsubsection{The Work Summary}
\label{sec:track-work-summary}

A table of periods says how much; the summary says how.  The
questions it answers are the ones a teacher asks about a term in
hindsight: did the hours add up, on which days were they done, how
long did the long days get, when does a working day usually start and
end, and where did the time go.  The answers are computed here, apart
from any printing, so that each can be tested from a handful of fixed
entries and so that the reports of \cref{sec:track-stats} only have to
decide what to say about them.

<<helper functions>>=
@dataclass
class WorkSummary:
    """Aggregate statistics over a window of tracked time.

    `longest_day` is a `(date, duration)` pair, `median_start` and
    `median_end` are times of day, and `top_label_sets` lists
    `(labels, duration)` pairs, largest first; all are `None` or
    empty when nothing was tracked.  `start_date` and `end_date`
    are the inclusive window the summary covers.
    """

    start_date: datetime.date
    end_date: datetime.date
    total: datetime.timedelta
    expected: datetime.timedelta
    workdays: int
    days_worked: int
    off_days_worked: int
    off_days_total: datetime.timedelta
    longest_day: tuple[datetime.date, datetime.timedelta] | None
    longest_streak: int
    median_start: datetime.time | None
    median_end: datetime.time | None
    top_label_sets: list[tuple[tuple[str, ...], datetime.timedelta]]

    @property
    def diff(self) -> datetime.timedelta:
        """Tracked minus expected time."""
        return self.total - self.expected

    @property
    def per_workday(self) -> datetime.timedelta | None:
        """Tracked time per work day, or `None` without work days."""
        if not self.workdays:
            return None
        return self.total / self.workdays
@

The computation starts from the same per-day totals the period tables
use, so the two can never disagree about the total.  [[end_date]] is
the window's end already clipped to today by the caller, for the same
reason as in [[expected_time]]: the work days still to come are not
yet owed.

<<helper functions>>=
def compute_work_summary(
    entries: "list[TrackingEntry]",
    start_date: datetime.date,
    end_date: datetime.date,
    daily_limit: float,
    work_days: list[str] | None = None,
) -> WorkSummary:
    """Summarise entries already clipped to `start_date`..`end_date`."""
    if work_days is None:
        work_days = get_work_days()
    daily_totals = compute_daily_wall_clock(entries)
    worked = sorted(
        date for date, duration in daily_totals.items()
        if duration > datetime.timedelta()
    )
    <<sum tracked time on work days and off days>>
    <<find the longest day and streak>>
    <<find the typical start and end of a day>>
    <<rank the label sets>>
    return WorkSummary(
        start_date=start_date,
        end_date=end_date,
        total=total,
        expected=expected_time(
            start_date, end_date, daily_limit, work_days
        ),
        workdays=count_work_days(start_date, end_date, work_days),
        days_worked=days_worked,
        off_days_worked=len(off_days),
        off_days_total=off_days_total,
        longest_day=longest_day,
        longest_streak=longest_streak,
        median_start=median_start,
        median_end=median_end,
        top_label_sets=top_label_sets,
    )
@

Work on a day the mask says is free is the deviation that a total
hides best: a week of 40 hours can be five even days or four short
ones and a Sunday.  So the days with time are split by the mask, and
the off days are counted and summed separately.

<<sum tracked time on work days and off days>>=
total = sum(daily_totals.values(), datetime.timedelta())
days_worked = sum(1 for date in worked if is_work_day(date, work_days))
off_days = [
    date for date in worked if not is_work_day(date, work_days)
]
off_days_total = sum(
    (daily_totals[date] for date in off_days), datetime.timedelta()
)
@

The longest day is the peak; the longest streak of consecutive days
with any tracked time is the other extreme, days without a break.
Both come from the sorted dates with time, so a day off in the middle
of a week ends a streak even if the week's total is unremarkable.

<<find the longest day and streak>>=
longest_day = None
if worked:
    longest_day = max(daily_totals.items(), key=lambda item: item[1])

longest_streak = 0
streak = 0
previous = None
for date in worked:
    if (
        previous is not None
        and date - previous == datetime.timedelta(days=1)
    ):
        streak += 1
    else:
        streak = 1
    longest_streak = max(longest_streak, streak)
    previous = date
@

A day's boundaries are the first start and the last end among the
entries that \emph{began} on that date, not among the midnight-split
intervals.  A session that runs past midnight belongs to the evening
it started in; splitting it would make the next day appear to start
at [[00:00]], which is a lie about when anyone sat down.  The end is
therefore measured from the start date's midnight and may exceed a
day, and is folded back to a time of day only for display, so that
such a session ends at [[01:12]] rather than at a 25th hour.

The medians, not the extremes, are what a \enquote{typical day}
means.  One 03:00 debugging session should not redefine when work
starts, and the median is indifferent to how extreme the outlier is.

<<find the typical start and end of a day>>=
first_starts = {}
last_ends = {}
for entry in entries:
    date = entry.start_time.date()
    midnight = datetime.datetime.combine(date, datetime.time.min)
    start = (entry.start_time - midnight).total_seconds()
    end = (entry.end_time - midnight).total_seconds()
    first_starts[date] = min(first_starts.get(date, start), start)
    last_ends[date] = max(last_ends.get(date, end), end)

median_start = median_end = None
if first_starts:
    median_start = time_of_day(
        statistics.median(first_starts.values())
    )
    median_end = time_of_day(statistics.median(last_ends.values()))
@

Folding a number of seconds back to a time of day is one line, but a
line with a modulo in it, so it gets a name.  Adding to
[[datetime.min]] lets [[datetime]] do the wrapping.

<<helper functions>>=
def time_of_day(seconds: float) -> datetime.time:
    """Fold seconds since some midnight to a time of day."""
    moment = datetime.datetime.min + datetime.timedelta(seconds=seconds)
    return moment.time().replace(microsecond=0)
@

The label sets are ranked the same way [[today]] groups them: by the
exact set, so that the tags stay peers and repeated bursts on the same
combination collapse into one line.  Summing the per-date label-set
totals across dates is exact for the reason given in
\cref{sec:track-calendar-periods}.  Three sets are enough to say where
the time went; a long tail would only repeat the table.

<<constants>>=
TOP_LABEL_SETS = 3
<<rank the label sets>>=
label_set_totals = {}
for label_totals in compute_daily_label_set_totals(entries).values():
    for labels_key, duration in label_totals.items():
        label_set_totals[labels_key] = (
            label_set_totals.get(labels_key, datetime.timedelta())
            + duration
        )
top_label_sets = sorted(
    label_set_totals.items(),
    key=lambda item: (-item[1].total_seconds(), item[0]),
)[:TOP_LABEL_SETS]
@

Let's verify every field from one fixed week, Monday 7 to Sunday 13
September 2026, with work on Monday to Wednesday and a Saturday:

<<test functions>>=
