\subsubsection{Relabeling an Active Batch}

The user who starts tracking with the wrong labels has, until now, two
bad options.  Stopping and restarting records a spurious short entry
and loses the original start time.  [[start --replace]] preserves the
start time via [[discard_labels]], but throws away any notes, and both
routes disturb state that has nothing to do with the mistake: a
detour through [[stop]] or [[switch]] rewrites [[last_stopped]] or the
[[switch_other]] toggle.

[[edit_batch]] instead performs the correction in place: it replaces
one batch's label set while keeping the batch's identity---its start
time, its notes, its [[batch_id]]---so nothing outside the batch
notices.  The optional [[start_time]] and [[notes]] parameters let the
same surgery also fix a wrong start or set the notes that
[[stop_labels]] turns into the entry's description when the batch is
eventually stopped.

We validate before mutating, for the same reason [[stop_labels]]
checks inverted intervals before popping: callers reach this method
through [[mutate_worker_tracking_state]], which persists whatever the
callback leaves behind, so a half-applied edit must never be
constructible.  Two things can be wrong with the requested label set.
An empty set would leave a batch that tracks nothing---the user wants
[[stop]], not [[edit]].  And a label that is already active in
\emph{another} batch would collide in [[active_labels]], silently
stealing the label from that batch; this method is the only place
labels join an \emph{existing} batch, so the one-batch-per-label
invariant is enforced here.

Removed labels do \emph{not} touch [[last_stopped]]: they were
corrected away, not stopped, and a later bare [[start]] should resume
what the user last \emph{stopped}, not a label they disowned as a
typo.  Added labels take the batch's \emph{existing} position in
[[label_order]] rather than appending to the end.  The invariant is
that an edit never changes which batch is most recent: correcting the
newest batch leaves it newest (so a bare [[stop]] right after still
ends the session that was just fixed), and correcting an \emph{older}
batch---possible via [[--containing]], perhaps from a background
script---must not make it jump the queue and silently retarget the
user's next bare [[stop]].

<<active session class>>=
    def edit_batch(
        self,
        batch_id: int,
        new_labels: list[str],
        start_time: datetime.datetime = None,
        notes: str = None,
    ) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
        """Replace the label set of one active batch in place.

        Kept labels retain their start time and notes unless
        `start_time` or `notes` is given, in which case every label
        of the batch gets the new value.  Added labels join the
        batch with its (possibly updated) start time and the given
        notes (empty if none).

        Returns:
            Tuple of (added, removed) label lists.

        Raises:
            ValueError: if the batch has no active labels, if
                `new_labels` is empty after de-duplication, or if a
                label to add is already active in another batch
        """
        old = [
            l for l in self.batches.get(batch_id, [])
            if l in self.active_labels
        ]
        <<validate the batch edit>>
        <<drop corrected-away labels>>
        <<rewrite kept labels in place>>
        <<attach added labels to the batch>>
        self.batches[batch_id] = kept + added
        return added, removed

