    ctx: typer.Context,
    labels: Annotated[list[str], labels_arg] = None,
    <<start command options>>
):
    """
    Start tracking labels, optionally tied to a command or tmux shell.

    Use this for everyday work such as starting a course/activity pair,
    correcting a late start, resuming the previous context, or launching a tool
    while the labels are active.

    \b
    Examples:
      nytid track start DD1310 lecture
      <<start command docstring examples>>
      nytid track start grading -- vim path/to/file
    """
    <<start command implementation>>
@

\subsubsection{Core Implementation}

The implementation handles loading the session, resolving labels (including 
resume functionality), calculating the start time, optionally preparing a
subprocess launch, and reporting results.

<<start command implementation>>=
<<resolve command argv>>
<<load session and resolve labels>>
<<calculate start time>>
<<prepare launch mode>>
<<start tracking and report results>>
<<launch subprocess if requested>>
@

The new pass-through form needs parsing before we decide whether an empty
label list means \enquote{resume the last session} or \enquote{launch only the
implicit command label}.  We therefore resolve the command argv exactly once
up front and let both the resume logic and the launch path reuse the same
result.

<<resolve command argv>>=
command_args = None
command_text = None
try:
    command_args, command_text = resolve_command_argv(command, ctx)
except ValueError as exc:
    typer.echo(f"Error parsing command: {exc}", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
except typer.BadParameter as exc:
    typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

We load the active session first only for read-only preflight decisions
such as resume and launch validation.  The actual state mutation happens
 later through [[start_tracking_labels()]], which reloads the worker's
 current session under lock before writing anything.  That keeps long-
 running composed commands from relying on stale in-memory session state.

<<load session and resolve labels>>=
session = load_active_session(who)

# If no labels provided, try to resume last stopped labels
resume_batches = False
if not labels:
    if command_args is not None:
        labels = []
    elif session.last_stopped:
        labels = session.last_stopped_labels()
        resume_batches = True
        typer.echo(
            f"Resuming: {', '.join(sorted(labels))}"
        )
    else:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: No labels specified and no "
            "previous labels to resume", err=True
        )
        typer.echo(
            "Usage: nytid track start "
            "<label1> [label2] ...", err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

After resolving labels, we start tracking and report what happened.
Any [[--replace]] handling is delegated to [[start_tracking_labels()]] so
the discard and restart happen against fresh on-disk state rather than
against this earlier snapshot.

When [[start]] launches a command or tmux shell, it may need to derive one
extra label from the command name and validate tmux usage.  When no
command was named, [[build_launch_shell]] picks the shell---plain tmux
or prompted direct---and seeds the prompt context as part of building
it.  An explicit command builds no shell, so there we seed the context
into its environment ourselves; it is harmless for a non-shell command
and lets an explicitly launched shell still show the marker.

Launch mode also needs a clear ownership rule for labels.  If a label is
already active and we launch a child process without saying what should
happen to the existing session, the command would appear to belong to that
label but the automatic shutdown path could leave the old session running.
We therefore require launch-bound labels to be inactive unless the user
explicitly picks one of two escape hatches: [[--replace]] (discard the old
session and restart) or [[--continue]] (keep the old session and bind the
child to it).  The shutdown path treats both cases identically---one
[[TrackingEntry]] per label at child exit---so the only difference is
whether the original start time survives.

The same rule must also cover the implicit label contributed by
[[--command]].  Otherwise a launch could smuggle in an already-active
command label after the preflight check and silently downgrade a conflict
to [[Already tracking]].

The effective launch label set should also be the one we report back to the
user.  If an explicit label matches the implicit command basename, keeping
the raw pre-deduplicated list would produce noisy output such as
[[Already tracking: echo, echo]] even though the session state correctly
tracks only one [[echo]] label.

Derived tmux work sometimes needs one more label source: the current
tracked context.  [[--inherit-labels]] reads labels from the current
tracked tmux window, or from the current tracked pane when no tracked
window owns that pane.  This is intentionally narrower than the worker's
full active session, so unrelated labels active elsewhere do not leak
into the derived launch.

Those inherited labels are already owned by the current tracked context,
so launch mode may borrow them without an explicit [[--continue]].  The
same exemption must not apply to unrelated implicit command labels,
which still need [[--replace]] unless the inherited context already
contains them.

Concretely: an implicit command label is exempted from [[--replace]]
only when it is part of the inherited context, not when it merely
matches a [[--continue]]-borrowed explicit label.  This is why the
conflict check for command labels filters on
[[inherited_context_labels]] directly rather than on the broader
[[borrowable_launch_labels]] set, while the explicit-label check uses
[[borrowable_launch_labels]] so [[--continue]] keeps working as before.

Track and todo should also present launch context the same way.  We therefore
compute one context string up front and reuse it for tmux window names and
as a best-effort [[PS1]] seed.  In tmux the user's [[~/.bashrc]] will
likely overwrite [[PS1]] during normal startup, so the tmux window name is
the primary context indicator for pane and window launches.

<<prepare launch mode>>=
launch_requested = (
    command_args is not None or pane or window
    or bool(wd_path) or bool(worktree)
)
tracking_labels = list(labels)
explicit_launch_labels = list(tracking_labels)
inherited_context_labels = []
borrowable_launch_labels = []
launch_owned_labels = list(tracking_labels)
launch_cleanup_labels = list(tracking_labels)
proc_env = None
proc_cwd = os.getcwd()
worktree_dir = None
prompt_file = None
prompt_dir = None
launch_context = None
window_name = None

if block is None:
    block = not (pane or window)

if not block and not (pane or window):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --no-block requires "
        "--pane or --window",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

if timeout is not None and command_args is None:
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --timeout requires --command",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

if launch_requested:
    try:
        validate_tmux_options(pane, window)
    except ValueError as exc:
        typer.echo(str(exc), err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    use_auto_suspend = should_auto_suspend(
        who, auto_suspend,
    )
    if use_auto_suspend and pane:
        warn_if_focus_events_off()

    <<resolve working directory>>
    proc_cwd = wd_path if wd_path else proc_cwd
    <<fork the launch into a worktree>>
    proc_env = os.environ.copy()

    if inherit_labels:
        inherited_context_labels = get_current_tracked_context_labels(
            who,
        )
        tracking_labels.extend(inherited_context_labels)

    if command_args is not None:
        command_label = os.path.basename(command_args[0])
        tracking_labels.append(command_label)

    launch_owned_labels = list(dict.fromkeys(
        tracking_labels
    ))
    borrowable_launch_labels = list(dict.fromkeys(
        inherited_context_labels
        + (
            explicit_launch_labels
            if continue_labels else []
        )
    ))

    launch_context = build_context_name(
        "track", launch_owned_labels
    )

    conflicting_explicit_labels = sorted({
        label for label in explicit_launch_labels
        if (
            session.get_label_info(label) is not None
            and label not in borrowable_launch_labels
        )
    })
    conflicting_command_labels = sorted({
        label for label in launch_owned_labels
        if (
            label not in explicit_launch_labels
            and label not in inherited_context_labels
            and session.get_label_info(label) is not None
        )
    })
    if conflicting_command_labels and not replace:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: launch mode requires inactive labels or "
            "--replace for implicit command labels: "
            f"{', '.join(conflicting_command_labels)}",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    if conflicting_explicit_labels and not (replace or continue_labels):
        typer.echo(
            "Error: launch mode requires inactive labels, "
            "--replace, or --continue: "
            f"{', '.join(conflicting_explicit_labels)}",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    if command_args is None:
        command_args, prompt_file, prompt_dir = \
            build_launch_shell(
                launch_context, proc_env,
                tmux=bool(pane or window),
            )
    else:
        apply_context_prompt(launch_context, proc_env)

    if window:
        window_name = launch_context
@

When a launched command is recorded in history, we store the full command
string as the batch description.  We attach it to every newly started label in
that batch so the grouped entry keeps the description regardless of which label
is encountered first during shutdown.

A plain bare [[start]] takes the batch-preserving resume path instead,
so a [[stop --all]] followed by [[start]] restores the stopped batches
rather than merging them into one.  Launch modes keep the flat path: a
new window or command legitimately claims the resumed labels as one
fresh batch of its own.  So does [[--replace]], whose discard-and-
restart semantics for still-active labels only exist on the flat path.

<<start tracking and report results>>=
if resume_batches and not launch_requested and not replace:
    started, already_tracking, discarded = (
        resume_stopped_batches(
            who=who,
            start_time=start_time,
        )
    )
else:
    started, already_tracking, discarded = start_tracking_labels(
        launch_owned_labels,
        start_time=start_time,
        who=who,
        replace=replace,
        description=command_text,
        update_tmux_context=not (pane or window),
    )

if discarded:
    typer.echo(
        f"Replaced: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(discarded))}"
    )

if started:
    typer.echo(
        f"Started tracking: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(started))}"
    )
if already_tracking:
    typer.echo(
        f"Already tracking: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(already_tracking))}"
    )
<<warn about ignored time options>>

borrowed_launch_labels = [
    label for label in already_tracking
    if label in borrowable_launch_labels
]
launch_cleanup_labels = list(dict.fromkeys(
    list(started) + borrowed_launch_labels
))

session = load_active_session(who)
all_active = session.get_active_labels()
typer.echo(f"Active labels: {', '.join(all_active)}")
if who != default_username:
    typer.echo(f"Worker: {who}")
<<report custom start time if used>>
@

\subsubsection{Time Offset Support}

Teachers often forget to start tracking immediately when they begin an activity.
The [[--offset]] option allows backdating the start time by a relative amount,
such as \enquote{I started 30 minutes ago.}
The [[offset_opt]] and [[at_opt]] definitions use generic wording because they
are reused by [[stop]], [[switch]], and [[add]]---each command interprets the
offset or time relative to its own action.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
offset_opt = typer.Option(
    "--offset", "-o",
    help="Offset current time "
         "(e.g., '-30m', '-1h30m', "
         "'90s', '30' for minutes)")
@

<<start command options>>=
offset: Annotated[str | None, offset_opt] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start DD1310 --offset -30m        # Forgot to start on time
@

\subsubsection{Absolute Time Support}

While offsets work well for recent activities, they become awkward for absolute 
times (\enquote{What's the offset from now to 2pm?}). The [[--at]] option 
specifies an exact time.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
at_opt = typer.Option(
    "--at",
    help="Override current time "
         "(e.g., '14:30' or '2024-12-13 14:30')")
@

<<start command options>>=
at: Annotated[str | None, at_opt] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start meeting --at 14:30          # Meeting began at 2:30 PM
nytid track start                             # Resume last stopped labels
nytid track start DD1310 --who Dan-Claude     # Track work for another worker
@

\subsubsection{Replacing an Already-Active Label}

Without [[--replace]], re-issuing [[track start labelA]] when [[labelA]]
is already active has no effect: the label is silently continued and
reported as \enquote{Already tracking}.  This is the safe default---a
typo does not corrupt history.

That safe default becomes a trap when the user also passed
[[--offset]] or [[--at]]: they explicitly asked for a different start
time, so \enquote{Already tracking} alone reads as success while the
requested backdate was quietly dropped.  We therefore warn on stderr
whenever already-active labels kept their original start time despite
an explicit time option, and point at [[--replace]] as the way to
actually move the start time.  A
warning rather than an error keeps the mixed case usable: when some
labels start fresh and others are already active, the command still
succeeds---and the time \emph{does} apply to the fresh ones.

<<warn about ignored time options>>=
if already_tracking and not replace \
        and (offset is not None or at is not None):
    ignored_opt = "--at" if at is not None else "--offset"
    typer.echo(
        f"Warning: {ignored_opt} ignored for already "
        f"tracked labels: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(already_tracking))}. "
        f"Use --replace to restart them at the "
        f"given time.",
        err=True,
    )
@

With [[--replace]], the active session for the named labels is discarded
first via [[discard_labels]], and those labels are then re-started at the
offset or absolute time.  The typical use case is time-correction: the
user forgot to start tracking and now wants to backdate the session.  The
abandoned entry never reaches the history file, so no cleanup is needed
afterwards.

The flag is only meaningful for labels that are already active; for labels
that are not yet tracked, [[--replace]] has no observable effect---it just
starts them normally.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
replace_opt = typer.Option(
    "--replace", "-r",
    help="If a label is already active, discard its "
         "current session and restart it from the "
         "given offset/time.")
@

<<start command options>>=
replace: Annotated[bool, replace_opt] = False,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start labelA --offset -60m --replace  # Restart active label earlier
@

\subsubsection{Continuing an Already-Active Label in a New Launch}

The [[--replace]] flag is the right answer when the user wants to
\emph{correct} the start time of an active label.  It is the
\emph{wrong} answer when the user just wants to keep working on the
same thing in a fresh tmux window or pane: the in-progress session
would be discarded, and the minutes already accumulated would
disappear.

The [[--continue]] flag covers that second case.  With [[--continue]],
launch mode (i.e.\ [[--window]], [[--pane]], or [[--command]]) accepts
explicitly requested labels that are already active: the existing
session is preserved untouched (no [[discard_labels]], no new history
entry, original start time unchanged), and the launched child process is
bound to the same labels via the normal window/pane-labels path.  When
the window or pane exits, the ordinary shutdown path stops those
explicitly continued labels together with any labels that this launch
started fresh, recording a single [[TrackingEntry]] covering the
original start time through the child's exit.

Implicit [[--command]] basename labels stay strict.  If the launched
command would implicitly add [[python]], [[echo]], or a similar basename
that is already active for unrelated work, [[--continue]] must not
silently borrow it: the later launch cleanup would otherwise stop
someone else's session.  We therefore let [[--continue]] bless only the
labels the user named explicitly, while implicit command labels still
require [[--replace]] when already active.

The two flags express different intents and are mutually exclusive in
spirit (though the code tolerates either one being set): pick
[[--replace]] to rewrite the start time, pick [[--continue]] to
preserve it.

Outside launch mode [[--continue]] is a no-op: the default behaviour
of [[track start]] on already-active labels is to silently continue
them and report \enquote{Already tracking}, so the flag has nothing
extra to do there.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
continue_opt = typer.Option(
    "--continue",
    help="If a label is already active, keep its "
         "current session and bind it to the new "
         "window/pane/command instead of failing.")
@

<<start command options>>=
continue_labels: Annotated[bool, continue_opt] = False,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start devel nytid -W --continue  # Bind active labels to new window
@

\subsubsection{Inheriting the Current Tracked Context}

Sometimes a new tmux window is not a fresh context but a branch from the
current one: while working on [[kthutils]], the user might discover that
[[weblogin]] needs a fix first.  The derived window should keep the
reason for the work---[[work]], [[devel]], and [[kthutils]]---while
adding the new label [[weblogin]].

The [[--inherit-labels]] flag covers that case.  In launch mode it reads
labels from the current tracked tmux context and adds them to the launch
label set before conflict checking and window naming.  If the current
pane belongs to a tracked window, that window's labels win; otherwise we
fall back to a tracked pane's labels.  Outside a tracked tmux context,
the flag is a no-op.  It is also silently ignored outside launch mode,
mirroring how [[--continue]] only takes effect when something is being
launched: with no child process to bind labels to, there is nothing to
inherit them into.

These inherited labels are treated like intentionally borrowed labels:
launch mode may reuse them even though they are already active, and the
later launch-cleanup path removes only this launch's contribution from
tracked tmux state.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
inherit_labels_opt = typer.Option(
    "--inherit-labels", "-I",
    help="In launch mode, add labels from the current tracked "
         "tmux pane/window context.")
@

<<start command options>>=
inherit_labels: Annotated[bool, inherit_labels_opt] = False,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start weblogin -W -I             # Branch from current tracked context
@

\subsubsection{Worker Identity}

The [[--who]] option identifies which worker is tracking. This enables
parallel tracking by different people (or agents) without interference,
since each worker maintains an independent active session file.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
who_opt = typer.Option(
    "--who", "-w",
    help="Worker identity (default: $USER)",
    autocompletion=complete_workers)
@

<<start command options>>=
who: Annotated[str, who_opt] = default_username,
@

\subsubsection{Launching a Command or a Tmux Shell}

Sometimes starting a label should immediately hand control to another tool:
mail in [[mutt]], a build in [[make]], or a shell in a dedicated tmux pane.
Rather than keeping a separate subcommand for that workflow, we let
[[start]] grow into it.

With the canonical [[-- prog args]] form, Click parses the tracking labels and
options first, then hands the remaining argv through untouched.  This is what
makes wrappers such as [[exec nytid track start -- /usr/bin/neomutt "$@"]]
work correctly even when one child argument contains spaces.

The legacy [[--command]] string form still works by running [[shlex.split]] on
the string.  In either form the command's basename becomes an additional
implicit label.  For example,
[[nytid track start work -- /usr/local/bin/neomutt -f inbox]] tracks both
[[work]] and [[neomutt]], while the full command string remains in the
description so the history still records what was launched.  With [[--pane]]
or [[--window]] but no command, [[start]] opens the user's default shell inside
tmux through the same plain-shell path as [[todo start]], so normal shell
startup still runs.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
command_opt = typer.Option(
    "--command", "-c",
    help="Legacy single-string command form "
         "(parsed with shlex; prefer `-- prog args`)")
@

<<start command options>>=
command: Annotated[str | None, command_opt] = None,
pane: Annotated[bool, pane_opt] = False,
window: Annotated[bool, window_opt] = False,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start email -- mutt              # Track email while launching mutt
nytid track start email -c "mutt"            # Legacy single-string form
nytid track start grading -P                  # Open a tracked shell in tmux pane
nytid track start admin -W                    # Open a tracked shell in tmux window
@

\subsubsection{Non-Blocking Tmux Launch}

By default, tmux launches return immediately and keep their labels tied
to the pane or window.  When the pane exits, a tmux hook stops those
same labels if they are still active.  [[--block]] instead waits for the
tmux session to finish and then performs the same label-based stop in the
foreground process.

<<start command options>>=
    block: Annotated[bool | None, block_opt] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start grading -P                 # Tracked pane, return immediately
nytid track start grading -P --block         # Wait for the pane to finish
@

\subsubsection{Auto-Suspend on Focus Change}

When a tmux pane loses focus (the user switches to another pane or
window), tracking continues even though the user is no longer working
in that context.  [[--auto-suspend]] installs [[pane-focus-out]] and
[[pane-focus-in]] hooks so that tracking pauses and resumes with the
pane's focus.  This produces many small completed entries rather than
one long one, but each entry accurately reflects \emph{attended} time.

The two launch modes differ in what counts as \enquote{leaving} the
tracked context.  With [[--pane]], tmux's [[pane-focus-out]] fires on
\emph{any} focus loss---including Alt-Tabbing to a browser---so pane
auto-suspend is aggressive.  With [[--window]], tracking follows the
tmux window's selection state rather than terminal focus: switching to a
browser does not suspend the session, only switching to another tmux
window does.  Use [[--window]] when looking things up in a browser is
part of the normal workflow.

The default is resolved through the config hierarchy documented in
\cref{track:configuration}: CLI flag overrides per-worker config,
which overrides global config, which defaults to true.

<<start command options>>=
    auto_suspend: Annotated[
        bool | None, auto_suspend_opt
    ] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start grading -P --no-auto-suspend  # No focus tracking
@

\subsubsection{Time-Boxed Tracking}

The [[--timeout]] option kills the subprocess after a specified duration,
preventing a forgotten session from accumulating hours.  It applies only
to direct subprocesses launched with [[--command]]---tmux panes are
interactive and the user can close them at will.

<<start command options>>=
timeout: Annotated[str | None, timeout_opt] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start grading -- vim --timeout 2h     # Stop after 2 hours
@

\subsubsection{Working Directory}

By default, a launched command runs in the caller's current directory
and inherits its environment.  The [[--wd]] option overrides the
working directory---useful when the tracking command is issued from a
different location than where the work should happen, for example
launching an editor inside a specific git repository:

<<start command options>>=
wd_path: Annotated[str | None,
                   wd_opt] = None,
@

Used \emph{alone}---with no command, [[--pane]], or [[--window]]---the
[[--wd]] option still launches something: a tracked shell in the
current terminal, rooted at the requested directory.  This is the
current-terminal counterpart of [[--window]], which opens that same
tracked shell in a new tmux window.  We cannot simply change directory
for the user, because [[nytid]] runs as a child of their shell and a
child can only change its own working directory, never its parent's.
Spawning a subshell at the target directory is the only way to drop the
user there while tracking runs, and tracking stops automatically when
they leave that shell with [[exit]].  The shell is built through
[[build_launch_shell]]---shared with [[todo start]]---which gives us a
prompt-prefixed shell here, so the prompt advertises the active
tracking context since without a tmux window name there is no other
visible indicator.

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start grading --wd ~/courses/DD1310 -- vim
nytid track start grading -C ~/courses/DD1310     # tracked shell there
@

The environment is always forwarded unconditionally to subprocesses,
so there is no [[--env]] flag here.  (The companion [[todo add]]
command \emph{does} offer [[--env]] and [[--here]] because it
persists the captured environment for later replay.)

We validate the path early so the user gets a clear error before
tracking starts rather than a confusing subprocess failure later:

<<resolve working directory>>=
if wd_path and not os.path.isdir(wd_path):
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: --wd directory does not "
        f"exist: {wd_path}",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Let us verify the bare-[[--wd]] behaviour.  With no command and no tmux
mode, [[--wd]] should still launch: a prompted shell rooted at the
requested directory, run in the current terminal.  We patch
[[subprocess.run]] so the shell is never really spawned and assert it
was asked to run a shell with the directory as its working directory.

<<test functions>>=
def test_start_wd_only_spawns_prompted_shell(
  temp_tracking_dir, tmp_path,
):
  """--wd alone launches a tracked shell in that directory."""
  with patch.dict(
    os.environ, {"SHELL": "/bin/bash"},
  ):
    with patch(
      "nytid.cli.track.subprocess.run",
    ) as mock_run:
      mock_run.return_value.returncode = 0
      result = runner.invoke(
        cli,
        ["start", "work", "-C", str(tmp_path)],
      )
  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert "Started tracking: work" in result.stdout
  mock_run.assert_called_once()
  call_kwargs = mock_run.call_args.kwargs
  assert call_kwargs["cwd"] == str(tmp_path)
  launched = mock_run.call_args.args[0]
  assert launched[0] == "/bin/bash"
  assert "--init-file" in launched
  # The announcement names the directory, not a tmux window,
  # since this shell opens in the current terminal.
  assert str(tmp_path) in result.stdout
  assert "tmux window" not in result.stdout
@

A bare-[[--wd]] launch now reaches the early directory check, so a
nonexistent path must fail before any tracking is recorded and before
any shell is spawned.

<<test functions>>=
def test_start_wd_nonexistent_errors_before_launch(
  temp_tracking_dir,
):
  """--wd with a missing directory errors before launching."""
  with patch(
    "nytid.cli.track.subprocess.run",
  ) as mock_run:
    result = runner.invoke(
      cli,
      ["start", "work", "-C", "/no/such/dir"],
    )
  assert result.exit_code == 1
  assert "directory does not exist" in result.output
  mock_run.assert_not_called()
@

On a shell we cannot write an init file for, the prompt marker comes
from the seeded environment alone.  The caller seeds it once, so the
forwarded [[PS1]] must carry exactly one context prefix---a regression
guard against the old double-application in the fallback path.

<<test functions>>=
def test_start_wd_only_seeds_single_prompt_prefix(
  temp_tracking_dir, tmp_path,
):
  """Fallback shells get exactly one context prefix in PS1."""
  with patch.dict(
    os.environ, {"SHELL": "/bin/sh"}, clear=True,
  ):
    with patch(
      "nytid.cli.track.subprocess.run",
    ) as mock_run:
      mock_run.return_value.returncode = 0
      result = runner.invoke(
        cli,
        ["start", "work", "-C", str(tmp_path)],
      )
  assert result.exit_code == 0
  env = mock_run.call_args.kwargs["env"]
  assert env["PS1"] == "track:work \\w\\n$ "
@

\subsubsection{Git Worktree}

The [[--worktree]] option (\cref{track:worktrees}) refines [[--wd]]:
the launch still starts from the [[--wd]] directory (or the current
one), but runs in the named worktree of the repository containing it.
Like a bare [[--wd]], a bare [[--worktree]] launches a tracked shell in
the current terminal---the user wants to \emph{be} in the worktree, and
a subshell is the only way to put them there.

<<start command options>>=
worktree: Annotated[str | None, worktree_opt] = None,
@

<<start command docstring examples>>=
nytid track start fix-42 -C ~/devel/nytid --worktree fix-42 -- claude
nytid track start fix-42 --worktree fix-42       # tracked shell there
@

The worktree is created (or reused) before tracking starts, right
after the [[--wd]] validation, so that a directory outside any
repository fails as cleanly as a nonexistent one.  We remember the
worktree directory separately from [[proc_cwd]]; the launch code
treats [[proc_cwd]] like any other working directory, and only the
cleanup step needs to know that it was a worktree.  The launch lands
in the same \emph{relative} place inside the worktree as [[--wd]]
was inside the repository (\cref{track:worktrees}), so
[[-C repo/src --worktree x]] runs in [[.../worktrees/x/src]] just as
the same command without [[--worktree]] runs in [[repo/src]].

<<fork the launch into a worktree>>=
if worktree:
    try:
        worktree_dir = ensure_worktree(proc_cwd, worktree)
    except ValueError as exc:
        typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    proc_cwd = worktree_launch_dir(proc_cwd, worktree_dir)
@

Let us verify that a bare [[--worktree]] launches the shell inside a
freshly created worktree, and that the unchanged worktree is removed
again once the shell exits.  The shell must not really run, but git
must, so the patched [[subprocess.run]] forwards [[git]] invocations to
the real function and mocks everything else.

<<test functions>>=
def run_only_git(*args, **kwargs):
  """Forward git subprocesses; mock everything else."""
  from unittest.mock import MagicMock
  if args and args[0] and args[0][0] == "git":
    return REAL_SUBPROCESS_RUN(*args, **kwargs)
  fake = MagicMock()
  fake.returncode = 0
  run_only_git.calls.append((args, kwargs))
  return fake


def test_start_worktree_alone_spawns_shell_in_worktree(
  temp_tracking_dir, tmp_path,
):
  """--worktree alone opens a tracked shell in the worktree."""
  repo = make_git_repo(tmp_path / "repo")
  run_only_git.calls = []
  with patch.dict(
    os.environ, {"SHELL": "/bin/bash"},
  ):
    with patch(
      "nytid.cli.track.subprocess.run",
      side_effect=run_only_git,
    ):
      result = runner.invoke(
        cli,
        ["start", "work", "-C", repo, "--worktree", "x"],
      )
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output
  expected = os.path.join(repo, ".claude", "worktrees", "x")
  assert expected in result.stdout
  assert len(run_only_git.calls) == 1
  args, kwargs = run_only_git.calls[0]
  assert kwargs["cwd"] == expected
  assert args[0][0] == "/bin/bash"
  # Nothing changed in the worktree, so it is removed again.
  assert not os.path.isdir(expected)
  assert "Removed unchanged worktree" in result.stdout


