Track and todo now share one argv-resolution helper because both commands need
the same compatibility rule: accept a literal [[--]] pass-through for exact
argv forwarding, keep the legacy single-string option working for old callers,
and reject attempts to mix the two forms in the same invocation.

<<helper functions>>=
class PassThroughCommand(typer.core.TyperCommand):
    """Typer command that captures argv after a literal ``--``."""

    def parse_args(self, ctx, args):
        trailing = None
        if "--" in args:
            idx = args.index("--")
            trailing = args[idx + 1:]
            args = args[:idx]
        if ctx.obj is None:
            ctx.obj = {}
        ctx.obj["trailing_argv"] = trailing
        return super().parse_args(ctx, args)


def resolve_command_argv(
    legacy_command_string: str | None,
    ctx: click.Context | None,
) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str | None]:
    """Resolve either legacy ``-c`` text or pass-through argv."""
    trailing = (ctx.obj or {}).get("trailing_argv") if ctx else None
    if legacy_command_string is not None and trailing is not None:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            'Use either -c "string" or -- prog args, not both.'
        )
    if trailing is not None:
        if not trailing:
            raise typer.BadParameter("Command cannot be empty")
        return list(trailing), shlex.join(trailing)
    if legacy_command_string is not None:
        argv = shlex.split(legacy_command_string)
        if not argv:
            raise typer.BadParameter("Command cannot be empty")
        return argv, legacy_command_string
    return None, None
@


Todo and track both describe shell context as a namespace plus the values
that identify the current work.  The same rendered string appears in
[[PS1]] and, for tmux windows, in the window name.  We therefore build
that context string once and let both commands reuse it.

<<helper functions>>=
def join_context_values(values: list[str]) -> str:
    """Return context values joined for prompts and tmux names."""
    return " ".join(values)


def build_context_name(
    prefix: str,
    values: list[str],
) -> str:
    """Return a prefixed context label for prompts and tmux names."""
    value_text = join_context_values(values)
    if value_text:
        return f"{prefix}:{value_text}"
    return prefix


def apply_context_prompt(
    context_name: str,
    env: dict[str, str],
) -> None:
    """Store a context marker in ``env`` for child shells."""
    if "PS1" in env:
        env["PS1"] = f"{context_name} {env['PS1']}"
    else:
        env["PS1"] = f"{context_name} \\w\\n$ "
@


Tmux shell launches need a different helper from direct interactive
subprocesses.  In tmux we want the user's normal shell startup path so
that [[~/.bashrc]], completion, and other interactive customisations load
exactly as they do in an ordinary window.  Todo and track therefore
share one helper that simply resolves the default shell command.

<<helper functions>>=
def build_plain_shell_command(
    env: dict[str, str],
) -> list[str]:
    """Return the plain shell command for interactive tmux launches."""
    shell = env.get("SHELL") or os.environ.get(
        "SHELL", "/bin/sh"
    )
    return [shell]
@


Direct interactive subprocesses still need one extra trick.
[[build_launch_shell]] has already seeded the context marker into
[[env]]'s [[PS1]] (the same single application the tmux path relies
on), but an interactive [[bash]] or [[zsh]] sources its init files at
startup and reassigns [[PS1]], discarding that seed.  So for those two
shells we write a temporary init file that sources the user's normal
startup and \emph{then} re-prepends the marker to the resulting
prompt---preserving completion and other interactive setup while making
the marker stick.

For any other shell we do not know the init-file convention, so we
leave the already-seeded [[PS1]] as the best effort and add nothing:
re-applying the marker here would double it, since
[[build_launch_shell]] seeded it once already.  Keeping the seed in
that one owner---rather than having every shell branch reapply it---is
what avoids the double prefix.

<<helper functions>>=
def build_prompted_shell_command(
    prefix: str,
    env: dict[str, str],
) -> tuple[list[str], str | None, str | None]:
    """Return a shell command with a prefixed prompt.

    ``env['PS1']`` is expected to already carry the context marker
    (``build_launch_shell`` seeds it); for bash and zsh we
    additionally write a temporary init file so an interactive
    startup does not discard it.
    """
    shell = env.get("SHELL") or os.environ.get(
        "SHELL", "/bin/sh"
    )
    shell_name = os.path.basename(shell)
    if shell_name == "bash":
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(
            mode="w", suffix=".sh", delete=False
        ) as f:
            f.write(
                "[ -f ~/.bashrc ] && source ~/.bashrc\n"
            )
            f.write(f'PS1="{prefix} $PS1"\n')
        return [shell, "--init-file", f.name], f.name, None

    if shell_name == "zsh":
        prompt_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp()
        with open(
            os.path.join(prompt_dir, ".zshrc"), "w"
        ) as f:
            f.write(
                "[ -f ~/.zshrc ] && source ~/.zshrc\n"
            )
            f.write(f'PS1="{prefix} $PS1"\n')
        env["ZDOTDIR"] = prompt_dir
        return [shell], None, prompt_dir

    return [shell], None, None
@


The two builders above answer the same question---\enquote{what shell
do we launch when the user named no command?}---but the choice between
them is identical everywhere it occurs: a tmux pane or window wants the
plain login shell, while a launch in the caller's own terminal wants
the prompt-prefixed shell.  Both [[track start]] and [[todo start]]
faced this fork, so we lift the rule into one place rather than letting
each command re-derive it.

Building the shell and marking its prompt are the same concern, so this
helper owns both: it seeds the context into [[env]]'s [[PS1]] and then
returns the shell, rather than trusting each caller to remember to seed
first.  That single seed is what every branch needs---the plain tmux
shell inherits it through the forwarded environment, and
[[build_prompted_shell_command]] makes it stick across an interactive
[[bash]] or [[zsh]] startup.  We return the same
[[(command, prompt_file, prompt_dir)]] triple as
[[build_prompted_shell_command]], with empty cleanup handles for the
tmux branch, which needs none.

<<helper functions>>=
def build_launch_shell(
    context_name: str,
    env: dict[str, str],
    *,
    tmux: bool,
) -> tuple[list[str], str | None, str | None]:
    """Return the shell command for a no-command launch.

    Seeds the context marker into ``env['PS1']``, then returns the
    shell: a plain login shell for ``tmux`` launches, or a
    prompt-prefixed shell for a direct launch in the caller's
    terminal.  Returns ``(command, prompt_file, prompt_dir)``; the
    latter two are ``None`` for the tmux case.
    """
    apply_context_prompt(context_name, env)
    if tmux:
        return build_plain_shell_command(env), None, None
    return build_prompted_shell_command(context_name, env)
@


Cleanup belongs next to construction because the temporary bash init
file or zsh [[ZDOTDIR]] directory must be removed by both callers.

<<helper functions>>=
def cleanup_prompted_shell_command(
    prompt_file: str | None,
    prompt_dir: str | None,
) -> None:
    """Remove temporary prompt artifacts for prefixed shells."""
    if prompt_file is not None:
        try:
            os.unlink(prompt_file)
        except OSError:
            pass

    if prompt_dir is not None:
        try:
            os.unlink(
                os.path.join(prompt_dir, ".zshrc")
            )
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            os.rmdir(prompt_dir)
        except OSError:
            pass


@


Detached tmux launches need two extra pieces of coordination beyond the
normal subprocess case.  First, each launch needs a unique channel name
so the parent can wait for the correct completion signal.  Second, the
child needs an atomic guard file so completion is recorded exactly once
even if multiple cleanup paths race.  The helper functions below provide
those two mechanisms.

The guard is a \emph{directory}, because [[mkdir]] is the one filesystem
operation every POSIX shell can perform atomically: it either creates
the directory or fails, and exactly one racer sees success.  Deriving
its path from the status file is what makes it disarmable.  A launch
whose parent does not stick around to clean up puts both files in one
throwaway directory; when the detached cleanup removes that directory,
a later [[mkdir]] of the guard fails with [[ENOENT]] rather than
succeeding on a fresh path---so releasing the guard and closing it
permanently are the same act.

<<helper functions>>=
def make_tmux_channel(prefix: str) -> str:
    """Return a unique tmux wait-for channel name."""
    return (
        f"nytid-{prefix}-"
        f"{os.getpid()}-{time.time_ns()}"
    )


def make_tmux_completion_guard(
    status_file: str | None,
) -> str:
    """Return the atomic completion guard path.

    Derived from the status file when there is one, so that
    removing the directory holding the status file also
    removes -- and disarms -- the guard.
    """
    if status_file is None:
        return os.path.join(
            tempfile.gettempdir(),
            f"nytid-guard-{os.getpid()}-{time.time_ns()}",
        )
    return status_file + ".guard"


def build_tmux_completion_shell(
    *,
    status_file: str | None = None,
    wait_channel: str | None = None,
    on_exit_shell: str | None = None,
    completion_guard: str | None = None,
    status_arg: str,
    signal_arg: str,
) -> str:
    """Return shell code for one-shot tmux completion."""
    shell_commands = []

    if status_file is not None:
        shell_commands.append(
            "printf 'status=%s\\nsignal=%s\\n' "
            f"{status_arg} {signal_arg} "
            f"> {shlex.quote(status_file)}"
        )
    if on_exit_shell is not None:
        shell_commands.append(on_exit_shell)
    if wait_channel is not None:
        shell_commands.append(
            "tmux wait-for -S " + shlex.quote(wait_channel)
        )

    body = "; ".join(shell_commands) or ":"
    if completion_guard is None:
        return body
    return (
        f"if mkdir {shlex.quote(completion_guard)} 2>/dev/null; then "
        f"{body}; "
        "fi"
    )


def build_tmux_wait_wrapper(
    command_args: list[str],
    ready_channel: str,
    *,
    status_file: str | None = None,
    wait_channel: str | None = None,
    on_exit_shell: str | None = None,
    completion_guard: str | None = None,
) -> list[str]:
    """Return a wrapper that waits and records normal exit."""
    script_parts = [
        f"tmux wait-for {shlex.quote(ready_channel)} || exit $?",
        '"$@"',
        "status=$?",
    ]
    completion_shell = build_tmux_completion_shell(
        status_file=status_file,
        wait_channel=wait_channel,
        on_exit_shell=on_exit_shell,
        completion_guard=completion_guard,
        status_arg='"$status"',
        signal_arg="''",
    )
    if completion_shell != ":":
        script_parts.append(completion_shell)
    script_parts.append('exit "$status"')
    return [
        "/bin/sh",
        "-c",
        "; ".join(script_parts),
        "sh",
    ] + command_args


def build_tmux_hook_command(
    *,
    status_file: str | None = None,
    wait_channel: str | None = None,
    on_exit_shell: str | None = None,
    completion_guard: str | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Build the tmux hook command for abnormal pane exit."""
    shell_command = build_tmux_completion_shell(
        status_file=status_file,
        wait_channel=wait_channel,
        on_exit_shell=on_exit_shell,
        completion_guard=completion_guard,
        status_arg=shlex.quote("#{pane_dead_status}"),
        signal_arg=shlex.quote("#{pane_dead_signal}"),
    )
    if shell_command == ":":
        return ""
    return "run-shell " + shlex.quote(shell_command)
@

When tmux splits a pane, the new pane inherits pane-scoped
options---including our exit hooks.  Without a guard, closing
\emph{any} descendant pane would stop tracking, even if the
original tracked pane is still alive.

The same inheritance bug affects [[new-window]]: creating a window
with \texttt{\^{}b c} while focused on the tracked pane causes the
new pane to inherit the exit hooks.  When that new window closes,
tmux has already selected a new active window (typically the tracked
one).  A format-string guard like [[#{==:#{pane_id},%42}]] fails
here because [[#{pane_id}]] resolves to the \emph{currently active}
pane---not the pane that triggered the event---so the guard
passes and tracking stops.

Instead of comparing pane IDs, we ask the semantically correct
question: is the tracked pane still alive?  We use
[[tmux display-message -t]] targeting the tracked pane; the command
fails (non-zero exit) when the pane no longer exists.  With the
leading~[[!]], the hook body only fires when the tracked pane is
dead.

This approach is correct across all exit paths:
\begin{description}
\item[Normal exit]  The wrapper script
  ([[build_tmux_wait_wrapper]]) runs the [[on_exit_shell]]
  \emph{before} the pane exits, so the wrapper handles cleanup.
  When the pane subsequently dies and the hook fires, the tracked
  pane is dead, so the guard lets the hook run---but
  [[_stop-detached]] uses [[quiet_if_missing]], making the
  duplicate invocation harmless.
\item[Abnormal exit]  When the pane is killed (e.g.\@ via
  [[kill-pane]]), the wrapper never executes its cleanup.  The
  [[pane-exited]]/[[pane-died]] hooks fire, the tracked pane is
  dead, and the guard lets the hook stop tracking.
\item[Spurious firing]  When a \emph{different} pane exits (via
  inheritance from [[split-window]] or the [[new-window]] bug),
  the tracked pane is still alive, so
  [[tmux display-message -t]] succeeds and the guard suppresses
  the hook.
\end{description}

For pane [[%42]] the resulting hook is:
\begin{quote}
\small [[if-shell '! tmux display-message -p -t %42 ""
  2>/dev/null' { run-shell '...' }]]
\end{quote}

<<helper functions>>=
def install_tmux_exit_hooks(
    pane_id: str,
    hook_command: str,
) -> None:
    """Install pane exit hooks for a newly created pane.

    The hook is wrapped in an ``if-shell`` guard that checks
    whether the tracked pane still exists, so that hooks
    inherited by split panes or (due to a tmux bug) by
    new-window panes are no-ops.
    """
    if not hook_command:
        return
    guarded_command = (
        "if-shell "
        f"'! tmux display-message -p -t {pane_id}"
        f' "" 2>/dev/null\' '
        f"{{ {hook_command} }}"
    )
    for hook_name in ("pane-exited", "pane-died"):
        subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "set-hook",
                "-p",
                "-t",
                pane_id,
                hook_name,
                guarded_command,
            ],
            check=True,
        )


def parse_tmux_exit_status(text: str) -> int:
    """Parse tmux hook status text into a shell exit code."""
    data = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if "=" not in line:
            continue
        key, value = line.split("=", 1)
        data[key] = value

    signal_str = data.get("signal", "")
    if signal_str:
        return 128 + int(signal_str)

    status_str = data.get("status", "")
    if status_str:
        return int(status_str)

    return 1


def read_tmux_exit_status(status_file: str) -> int:
    """Read the exit code recorded by the tmux hook."""
    try:
        with open(status_file) as f:
            return parse_tmux_exit_status(f.read())
    except OSError:
        return 1


def run_tmux_command(
    command_args: list[str],
    *,
    cwd: str,
    env: dict[str, str],
    window: bool = False,
    window_name: str | None = None,
    block: bool = True,
    exit_status_file: str | None = None,
    on_exit_shell: str | None = None,
    focus_labels: list[str] | None = None,
    focus_who: str | None = None,
    focus_window: bool = False,
    auto_suspend: bool = True,
) -> int | None:
    """Run a command in tmux and optionally wait for exit.

    When ``focus_labels`` is set, they are published as the new
    pane's or window's tracked context, which is what lets
    nested commands discover the labels they run under.

    ``auto_suspend`` additionally arms the hooks that stop and
    restart those labels when attention moves.  Pane launches
    suspend directly on pane focus changes, while window
    launches synchronize against the authoritative tmux
    selected window.
    """
    ready_channel = make_tmux_channel("ready")
    wait_channel = None
    status_file = exit_status_file
    pane_id = None
    window_id = None
    session_id = None
    remove_status_file = False
    completion_guard = None
    tmux_server_key = None

    if block and status_file is None:
        fd, status_file = tempfile.mkstemp(
            prefix="nytid-tmux-",
            suffix=".status",
        )
        os.close(fd)
        remove_status_file = True
    if block:
        wait_channel = make_tmux_channel("done")
    if status_file is not None or on_exit_shell is not None:
        completion_guard = make_tmux_completion_guard(
            status_file
        )

    wrapped_command = build_tmux_wait_wrapper(
        command_args,
        ready_channel,
        status_file=status_file,
        wait_channel=wait_channel,
        on_exit_shell=on_exit_shell,
        completion_guard=completion_guard,
    )

    hook_command = build_tmux_hook_command(
        status_file=status_file,
        wait_channel=wait_channel,
        on_exit_shell=on_exit_shell,
        completion_guard=completion_guard,
    )

    tmux_args = [
        "tmux",
        "new-window" if window else "split-window",
        "-P",
        "-F",
        "#{pane_id} #{window_id} #{session_id}",
    ]
    for key, value in sorted(env.items()):
        tmux_args.extend([
            "-e",
            f"{key}={value}",
        ])
    if window and window_name:
        tmux_args.extend(["-n", window_name])
    tmux_args.extend([
        "-c",
        cwd,
    ])
    tmux_args.append(shlex.join(wrapped_command))

    old_sigint = None
    if block:
        old_sigint = signal.signal(
            signal.SIGINT, signal.SIG_IGN
        )

    try:
        tmux_server_key = compute_tmux_server_key(
            env.get("TMUX")
        )
        if tmux_server_key:
            migrate_tmux_state_files(tmux_server_key)
        <<launch tmux command and install hooks>>
        subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "wait-for", "-S", ready_channel],
            check=True,
        )
        if not block:
            return None
        subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "wait-for", wait_channel],
            check=True,
        )
        return read_tmux_exit_status(status_file)
    except Exception:
        if pane_id:
            try:
                subprocess.run(
                    ["tmux", "kill-pane", "-t", pane_id],
                    check=False,
                )
            except OSError:
                pass
        raise
    finally:
        if block and old_sigint is not None:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, old_sigint)
        if remove_status_file and status_file is not None:
            try:
                os.unlink(status_file)
            except OSError:
                pass
        if block and completion_guard is not None:
            try:
                os.rmdir(completion_guard)
            except OSError:
                pass
@

Two details of the setup above are easy to get wrong, and getting either
wrong breaks the exactly-once invariant.

The guard is built whenever there is a status file \emph{or} an
[[on_exit_shell]], not only when we block.  Both the pane wrapper and
the [[pane-exited]]/[[pane-died]] hooks are handed the same
[[completion_guard]], and [[build_tmux_completion_shell]] emits an
unguarded body when it is [[None]].  Tying the guard to [[block]] left
detached launches---every [[--no-block]] pane or window, and every
detached [[todo start]]---running their finalize command twice: once
from the wrapper as the child exits, once again from the hook after the
pane dies.  [[_stop-detached]] tolerates that, but the todo finalize
does not.

The parent only tears the guard down when it blocked.  Having waited for
the completion signal, it knows the guard has served its purpose.  A
detached parent knows nothing of the sort: it returns while the pane is
still alive, so removing the guard then would simply hand the later
hooks a clean slate to re-claim.  Detached launches instead let their
own cleanup remove the throwaway directory that holds guard and status
alike (see \cref{track:detached-launch-dir}).

A tracked launch is also the natural moment to repair state written
by an older nytid under a wrong or outdated server key---files that
this server's windows still own but that today's key computation can
no longer address (\cref{track:server-sweep}).  The migration only
renames files, never deletes, and it costs nothing in the common
case: with no candidate files on disk it does not even query tmux.
It runs outside [[with_tmux_context_lock]] deliberately: the files
it moves are unaddressable under the current key, so no live hook
reads them, and keeping it outside the lock avoids nesting with the
hook-observability critical section below.

Tracked window launches need one stronger serialization guarantee than
steady-state hooks.  The [[tmux new-window]] command itself may spawn a
[[session-window-changed]] hook before we have written the new window's
labels and updated [[last_selected_window]].  We therefore hold the
tmux-context lock across a narrow \enquote{hook-observability} phase
that covers [[new-window]], label publication, and the
[[last_selected_window]] write.

The lock begins before invoking [[tmux new-window]] so any detached
hook spawned by that command is forced to wait before it can snapshot
tmux-context state.  We release the lock as soon as
[[last_selected_window]] names the new window.  From that point on, the
detached switch command sees one coherent source/target pair and can
discard any redundant [[prev == selected]] event.

The actual stop/start transition runs \emph{after} the lock has been
released.  That split is deliberate: the lock protects only the state
that hooks observe, while the tracking-state mutation happens from the
captured labels outside the critical section.  Hook installation also
stays outside the lock because [[tmux set-hook]] publishes no tracked
state of its own.

