@

\subsubsection{Per-Window Active Labels}
\label{track:window-auto-suspend}

Window auto-suspend has different semantics from pane auto-suspend.
Instead of following terminal focus, it follows whether the tracked tmux
window is selected by any attached client.  To support that, we keep a
window-scoped labels file analogous to the pane labels file.  As with
pane state, the filename includes the tmux server key so two different
tmux server instances sharing a filesystem do not reuse the same
window-state path for identical [[@...]] identifiers.  The instance
distinction matters when a server is killed and restarted without a
reboot: the new server reuses the same socket path \emph{and}
restarts window numbering at [[@0]], so only the generation part of
the key keeps the dead server's files from being mistaken for the
new server's windows (\cref{track:server-key}).  A reboot instead
clears the runtime state directory itself
(\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}).

<<helper functions>>=
def window_labels_path(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return path to a tracked window's labels file."""
    safe_window = window_id.replace("@", "window_")
    return (
        get_tmux_state_dir()
        / f"window_labels_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}_{safe_window}"
        f"_{sanitize_who(who)}.json"
    )
@

Tmux's [[session-window-changed]] hook runs the switch command via
[[run-shell]], which spawns an asynchronous subprocess.  By the time
that subprocess queries tmux for the \enquote{last window} (via
[[$0:!]]), the pointer has already advanced---empirically, both
[[#{window_id}]] and [[$0:!]] resolve to the same window after the
switch.  We therefore maintain our own state file that records the
last selected window per session and worker.  Each invocation of
[[window_switch_detached]] reads the previous value (the source
window) and writes the current value, giving the next invocation
a reliable baseline.

<<helper functions>>=
def last_selected_window_path(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
) -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return path to the last-selected-window state file."""
    return (
        get_tmux_state_dir()
        / f"last_window"
        f"_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}"
        f"_{sanitize_tmux_id(session_id)}"
        f"_{sanitize_who(who)}.txt"
    )


def read_last_selected_window(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Read the last-selected window ID, or ``None``."""
    path = last_selected_window_path(
        who, tmux_server_key, session_id,
    )
    try:
        text = path.read_text().strip()
        return text if text else None
    except OSError:
        return None


def write_last_selected_window(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
    window_id: str,
) -> None:
    """Record the currently selected window ID."""
    path = last_selected_window_path(
        who, tmux_server_key, session_id,
    )
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(window_id + "\n")
    tmp.rename(path)
@

We reuse the same absent-versus-empty distinction as for panes:
[[None]] means the window is not tracked, while an empty visible label
set means the tracked file exists but currently has no labels.

Preserving that distinction is what keeps a window tracked across a full
stop.  When the user stops every label in a still-open window the count
dict drains to empty, but the window has not gone away: the file must
survive as an empty marker so a later resume re-attaches its labels to
the window instead of starting them as global, window-less labels.  We
therefore return the empty dict verbatim and reserve [[None]] for a
genuinely absent file.

Unlike pane state, window state may receive overlapping contributions.
Two panes in the same tracked window can both contribute [[work]], and a
single pane exit must not delete [[work]] globally while the other pane
still owns it.  We therefore persist per-label reference counts on disk.

Besides the count, each label also records the id of the batch it
belongs to in the active session.  The batch id is what lets a bare
[[stop]] or hashless [[edit]] inside a tracked window target only that
window's batches (\cref{track:window-batch-scope}): the window file acts
as a candidate filter over the session's batches, never as a source of
truth.  Recording the \emph{id} rather than relying on label names is
what makes stale files harmless---when a batch is stopped its id never
recurs ([[next_batch_id]] only grows), so a leftover entry simply
matches nothing, whereas a recycled label \emph{name} would falsely
claim a brand-new global batch for the window.

The on-disk value for a label has gone through three generations: a
flat list (each label count~1, no batch), a label-to-count dict (no
batch), and now a label-to-entry dict where each entry carries
[[count]] and [[batch]] ([[None]] when unknown).  These files live in
the tmux runtime state directory and vanish on reboot, so migration is
read-side only: one canonical reader accepts all three generations and
the writers only ever emit the newest.

<<helper functions>>=
def read_window_label_entries(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> dict[str, dict] | None:
    """Read tracked window label entries, or ``None`` if absent.

    Each entry maps a label to
    ``{"count": int, "batch": int | None}``.
    """
    path = window_labels_path(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return None

    if isinstance(data, list):
        return {
            label: {"count": 1, "batch": None}
            for label in dict.fromkeys(data)
        }

    if isinstance(data, dict):
        entries = {}
        for label, value in data.items():
            <<normalize one window label entry>>
        return entries

    return None
@

Within a dict-format file the two dict generations may in principle mix
(they never do in practice, but accepting both per value costs
nothing).  Any malformed value is dropped rather than propagated.

<<normalize one window label entry>>=
if isinstance(value, int):
    if value > 0:
        entries[label] = {
            "count": value, "batch": None,
        }
elif isinstance(value, dict):
    count = value.get("count")
    if isinstance(count, int) and count > 0:
        batch = value.get("batch")
        entries[label] = {
            "count": count,
            "batch": (
                batch
                if isinstance(batch, int)
                else None
            ),
        }
@

The count and batch views are simple projections of the entries.  The
count view keeps its historical name and shape because tests and the
removal logic think in reference counts; the batch view is what the
window-scoped selection consumes.

<<helper functions>>=
def read_window_label_counts(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> dict[str, int] | None:
    """Read tracked window label counts, or ``None`` if absent."""
    entries = read_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if entries is None:
        return None
    return {
        label: entry["count"]
        for label, entry in entries.items()
    }


def read_window_label_batches(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> dict[str, int | None] | None:
    """Read tracked window batch ids, or ``None`` if absent."""
    entries = read_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if entries is None:
        return None
    return {
        label: entry["batch"]
        for label, entry in entries.items()
    }

<<helper functions>>=
def read_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> list[str] | None:
    """Read tracked window labels, or ``None`` if absent."""
    counts = read_window_label_counts(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if counts is None:
        return None
    return list(counts)
@

Writing again uses an atomic temporary file in the same directory.
[[write_window_labels]] keeps its plain-list signature because its
callers (window launch, test seeding) run before any session mutation
and so cannot know batch ids yet; the ids are backfilled once the
session has allocated them (see [[update_window_label_batches]]
below).

<<helper functions>>=
def write_window_label_entries(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    entries: dict[str, dict],
) -> None:
    """Atomically write a tracked window's label entries."""
    path = window_labels_path(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(entries))
    tmp.rename(path)


def write_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
) -> None:
    """Atomically write a tracked window's labels."""
    write_window_label_entries(
        window_id,
        who,
        tmux_server_key,
        {
            label: {"count": 1, "batch": None}
            for label in dict.fromkeys(labels)
        },
    )
@

As with panes, we merge new labels while preserving the visible order.
Repeated contributions increment the label's reference count instead of
duplicating it in the visible list.  When the caller knows batch ids
(registration runs inside the session mutation, where the session is at
hand), it passes them in [[batches]] and they overwrite whatever the
file recorded before: a label being (re)started or relabeled has just
been placed in a batch, so the session's current assignment is the
truth and any older id is stale by definition.

<<helper functions>>=
def add_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
    batches: dict[str, int] | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Add labels to a tracked window's active set."""
    existing = read_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    ) or {}
    for lbl in labels:
        if lbl not in existing:
            existing[lbl] = {"count": 0, "batch": None}
        existing[lbl]["count"] += 1
        if batches and lbl in batches:
            existing[lbl]["batch"] = batches[lbl]
    write_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key, existing,
    )
@

Some call sites write the window file \emph{before} the session
mutation that allocates batch ids: the window launch publishes the file
immediately so the tmux hook subprocesses see it, and [[adopt]] merges
its labels under the tmux-context lock before starting them.  Those
sites backfill the ids afterwards.  The backfill touches only labels
the file already lists and leaves counts alone---it corrects the batch
view without disturbing the reference-count bookkeeping, and a file
that disappeared in between (window closed) is silently left absent.

<<helper functions>>=
def update_window_label_batches(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    batches: dict[str, int],
) -> None:
    """Backfill batch ids for a window's existing labels."""
    existing = read_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if existing is None:
        return
    for lbl, batch_id in batches.items():
        if lbl in existing:
            existing[lbl]["batch"] = batch_id
    write_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key, existing,
    )
@

Removing labels decrements the corresponding reference counts and only
drops a visible label once the last contributing pane has gone away.
Draining the dict to empty is deliberately \emph{not} a deletion: an
empty count dict means \enquote{no labels are active right now}, which is
not the same fact as \enquote{this window is gone}.  A user can stop
every label in a window that is still open and expect to resume them
there later, so we always write the (possibly empty) dict back and leave
the file in place as the window's tracked marker.  Genuine teardown ---
deleting the file once the window itself disappears --- lives in the
pane-exit cleanup command, which can ask tmux whether the window still
exists (see \cref{track:detached-finalize}).

<<helper functions>>=
def remove_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    labels: list[str],
) -> None:
    """Remove labels from a tracked window's active set."""
    existing = read_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if existing is None:
        return
    for label in dict.fromkeys(labels):
        entry = existing.get(label)
        if entry is None:
            continue
        if entry["count"] <= 1:
            del existing[label]
        else:
            entry["count"] -= 1
    write_window_label_entries(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key, existing,
    )
@

Because [[remove_window_labels]] no longer deletes, the only caller that
removes a window file outright is the pane-exit cleanup command, once it
has confirmed the window itself is gone.  We keep that unlink in its own
idempotent helper so the cleanup path can call it unconditionally without
caring whether the file was already absent.

<<helper functions>>=
def delete_window_labels(
    window_id: str,
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> None:
    """Remove a tracked window's labels file."""
    try:
        window_labels_path(
            window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
        ).unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
@

\subsubsection{Window-Scoped Batch Targeting}
\label{track:window-batch-scope}

A tracked window is a promise of isolation: work started elsewhere ---
another terminal, a script, a different window --- must not become what
a bare [[stop]] or hashless [[edit]] \emph{in this window} acts on.
Without that promise, every bare command races against concurrent
tracking: whichever batch started last wins the \enquote{most recent}
crown, wherever it came from.  The batch ids recorded in the window
file (\cref{track:window-auto-suspend}) close the race: they name the
batches that belong to this window, and the selection in
[[most_recent_batch_labels]] restricts itself to them.

The distinction between \emph{no} tracked window and an \emph{empty}
one carries through to the scope.  [[None]] means the command runs
outside any tracked window, so selection stays global---plain shells
keep today's behavior.  An empty set means the window is tracked but
nothing is tracking in it (the user stopped everything and is taking a
break); a bare [[stop]] then finds nothing rather than reaching out
and grabbing a global batch, which is exactly the isolation the window
promised.

Legacy entries without a batch id resolve by name through the
session's [[active_labels]]---if the label is still active somewhere,
its current batch stands in for the unrecorded one.  This is the safe
direction for the fallback: a recycled name at worst \emph{includes} a
batch in the window's scope, and only until the id-carrying rewrite on
the next registration.

The helper reads the window file, so it requires the tmux-context lock;
its callers are the [[update]] closures run by
[[mutate_worker_tracking_state]], which holds that lock throughout.

<<helper functions>>=
def current_window_batch_scope(
    session,
    who: str,
) -> set[int] | None:
    """Return the tracked window's batch-id scope, or ``None``.

    ``None`` means no tracked window is current (selection stays
    global); an empty set means the window is tracked but has no
    batches.  Requires the tmux-context lock to already be held.
    """
    tmux_server_key = get_current_tmux_server_key()
    if tmux_server_key is None:
        return None
    window_id = get_current_tracked_window_id(
        who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if not window_id:
        return None
    batches = read_window_label_batches(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    ) or {}
    scope = set()
    for label, batch_id in batches.items():
        if batch_id is not None:
            scope.add(batch_id)
        elif label in session.active_labels:
            scope.add(session.active_labels[label][2])
    return scope
@

The inverse question---\enquote{which window does this batch belong
to?}---arises when a stop snapshots the resume set: the answer lets a
later bare [[start]] re-attach each resumed batch to its window.  The
lookup keys on batch ids, not label names, because two tracked windows
may legitimately declare the same label (that is why the files hold
reference counts); the batch id is unique across the session.  It must
run \emph{before} the stop's [[post_update]] drains the current
window's file, which is exactly the order
[[mutate_worker_tracking_state]] gives its [[update]] callback.
Outside tmux there is nothing to consult and the groups stay
window-less, degrading resume to today's current-context behavior.

<<helper functions>>=
def annotate_last_stopped_windows(
    session,
    who: str,
) -> None:
    """Stamp last_stopped groups with their owning windows.

    Requires the tmux-context lock to already be held.
    """
    tmux_server_key = get_current_tmux_server_key()
    if tmux_server_key is None:
        return
    batch_windows = {}
    pattern = (
        "window_labels"
        f"_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}"
        f"_window_*_{sanitize_who(who)}.json"
    )
    for path in get_tmux_state_dir().glob(pattern):
        match = _STATE_FILE_ID_RE.search(path.name)
        if not match:
            continue
        window_id = f"@{match.group(2)}"
        batches = read_window_label_batches(
            window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
        ) or {}
        for batch_id in batches.values():
            if batch_id is not None:
                batch_windows[batch_id] = window_id
    for group in session.last_stopped:
        window_id = batch_windows.get(group.get("batch"))
        if window_id is not None:
            group["window"] = window_id
            group["server_key"] = tmux_server_key
@

The three scope shapes---untracked, tracked-but-empty, and populated
(with a legacy batchless entry resolved by name)---are each worth a
check:

<<test functions>>=
def test_current_window_batch_scope(temp_tracking_dir):
  """Scope is None untracked, empty on break, ids otherwise."""
  window_id = "@42"
  session = ActiveSession()
  t0 = datetime.datetime.now()
  session.start_labels(["a"], t0)
  session.start_labels(["b"], t0)
  a_id = session.active_labels["a"][2]
  b_id = session.active_labels["b"][2]
  env = {
    "TMUX_PANE": "%99",
    "TMUX": "/tmp/tmux.sock,123,0",
  }
  with patch.dict(os.environ, {}, clear=True):
    assert current_window_batch_scope(
      session, default_username,
    ) is None
  with patch.dict(os.environ, env), patch(
    "nytid.cli.track.get_tmux_window_id_for_pane",
    return_value=window_id,
  ):
    assert current_window_batch_scope(
      session, default_username,
    ) is None
    write_window_labels(
      window_id, default_username, TEST_SERVER_KEY, [],
    )
    assert current_window_batch_scope(
      session, default_username,
    ) == set()
    add_window_labels(
      window_id, default_username, TEST_SERVER_KEY,
      ["a"], batches={"a": a_id},
    )
    add_window_labels(
      window_id, default_username, TEST_SERVER_KEY,
      ["b"],
    )
    assert current_window_batch_scope(
      session, default_username,
    ) == {a_id, b_id}
@

End to end, the promise reads: a batch started in a plain shell after
the window's batch must not be what a bare [[stop]] or hashless
[[edit]] in the window acts on---and during a break the bare commands
find nothing rather than reaching for the global batch.  An explicit
[[--containing]], by contrast, crosses the window boundary on purpose.

