\subsubsection{Window Transition Execution}
\label{track:event-buffer}

Window tracking no longer reconstructs transitions from deferred focus
events.  Instead, [[session-window-changed]] is authoritative: whenever
tmux reports a newly selected window, the detached command reads the
previously selected window from [[last_selected_window]], snapshots the
labels attached to both windows under [[with_tmux_context_lock]], writes
the new authoritative value, and then performs the stop/start transition
from that captured state after releasing the lock.

That invariant solves the terminal-blur problem directly.  Alt-Tab away
from the terminal does not change tmux's selected window, so no
[[session-window-changed]] event fires and no transition runs.  Only an
actual tmux window selection change can stop or start tracked labels.

The lock exists only to serialize hook-observable tmux context files.
The potentially slower tracking mutations happen outside the critical
section using [[update_tmux_context=False]], because the window-label and
[[last_selected_window]] files are already correct by the time those
mutations begin.

When debugging unexpected history splits, we still want one durable log
line per executed transition.  When [[track.debug]] is truthy, nytid
appends one line per executed window transition to a debug log in the
tracking directory and also emits a best-effort desktop notification via
[[notify-send]].  The notification path must not affect tracking
behaviour, so missing [[notify-send]] is silently ignored.

Desktop notifications are inherently less reliable than the file log,
because tmux hook subprocesses only inherit whatever graphical session
environment the server can see.  The file log is therefore the primary
record, while notifications are a convenience for live observation.

Because the setting lives in nytid's configuration store rather than one
shell environment, tmux hooks see the same debug flag as interactive
commands.  That makes the instrumentation reliable across both the live
CLI and detached hook processes.

Tmux hook subprocesses also read pane/window context files without going
through the main tracking-state mutation helper.  We therefore guard
those files with their own lock so hook readers and command-side writers
see one coherent view.

<<helper functions>>=
def with_tmux_context_lock(callback):
    """Run ``callback`` while holding the tmux context lock."""
    lock_file = get_tmux_context_lock_file()
    with open(lock_file, "w") as lock_handle:
        fcntl.flock(lock_handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            return callback()
        finally:
            fcntl.flock(lock_handle, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
@

The debug flag is only used by the window-transition diagnostics, so we
introduce [[TRACK_DEBUG_CONFIG]] immediately before that helper.

<<constants>>=
TRACK_DEBUG_CONFIG = "track.debug"
@

The debug flag check reads [[track.debug]] from nytid's configuration
store so that both interactive commands and detached hooks see the same
value.

<<helper functions>>=
def track_debug_enabled() -> bool:
    """Return whether ``track.debug`` enables hook tracing."""
    try:
        value = config.get(TRACK_DEBUG_CONFIG)
    except KeyError:
        return False
    return str(value).strip().lower() in {
        "true", "1", "yes",
    }
@

The debug log collects executed window transitions in one file,
providing a chronological record across detached tmux hooks.

<<helper functions>>=
def window_event_debug_log_path() -> pathlib.Path:
    """Return the debug log path for window transitions."""
    return get_tmux_state_dir() / "window_event_debug.log"
@

The diagnostics become clearer if they report the actual transition we
executed rather than the intermediate state we observed while deciding.

<<helper functions>>=
def summarize_window_transition(
    source_window_id: str | None,
    source_labels: list[str] | None,
    target_window_id: str | None,
    target_labels: list[str] | None,
) -> str:
    """Return a one-line summary of an executed window transition."""
    source_text = source_window_id or "(none)"
    target_text = target_window_id or "(none)"
    stop_text = " ".join(source_labels or []) or "(none)"
    start_text = " ".join(target_labels or []) or "(none)"
    return (
        f"source={source_text} stop={stop_text} "
        f"target={target_text} start={start_text}"
    )
@

The main diagnostic emitter writes to the log file and optionally sends
a desktop notification via [[notify-send]].  Missing [[notify-send]] is
silently ignored because the file log is the primary record.

<<helper functions>>=
def emit_window_event_debug(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
    session_id: str,
    action: str,
    event_line: str,
    summary_text: str = "",
    notify: bool = True,
    notification_body: str | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Emit env-gated diagnostics for executed window transitions."""
    if not track_debug_enabled():
        return

    timestamp = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat(sep=" ")
    summary = summary_text.strip() or "(none)"
    log_line = (
        f"{timestamp} pid={os.getpid()} who={who} "
        f"server={tmux_server_key} session={session_id} "
        f"action={action} event={event_line!r} "
        f"summary={summary!r}\n"
    )
    with open(window_event_debug_log_path(), "a") as handle:
        handle.write(log_line)

    if not notify:
        return

    body = notification_body or (
        f"action: {action}\n"
        f"event: {event_line}\n"
        f"summary: {summary}\n"
        f"who: {who}\n"
        f"session: {session_id}"
    )
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            ["notify-send", "nytid track debug", body],
            check=False,
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            env=os.environ.copy(),
        )
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
@

\subsubsection{Suspend and Resume Helpers}

The suspend helper stops only the labels that are currently active
in the worker's session---any label already stopped (by the user
or another process) is silently skipped.  The subtle part is that
this filtering must happen inside the same locked mutation that
performs the stop.  A pre-lock snapshot can go stale between the
read and the write, leaving the suspend state file claiming that a
label was paused even though another command had already stopped it.

We therefore delegate to [[stop_tracking_labels_if_active()]] and
persist only the subset that helper actually stopped.  The state file
then records the truth rather than the caller's optimistic assumption.

<<helper functions>>=
def suspend_tracking_labels(
    who: str,
    labels: list[str],
    pane_id: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> None:
    """Suspend tracking for a pane that lost focus.

    Stops whichever of ``labels`` are still active and
    writes them to the pane's suspend state file.
    """
    _, _, stopped_labels = stop_tracking_labels_if_active(
        who=who,
        labels=labels,
        update_tmux_context=False,
    )
    if not stopped_labels:
        return
    state_path = suspend_state_path(
        pane_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    write_suspend_state(state_path, stopped_labels)
@

We delete the state file \emph{before} restarting labels so that a
crash between the two steps never causes a double-resume---losing
an unfocused session is preferable to silently double-booking time.
If the file is absent (pane exited while unfocused, or user manually
stopped everything), the resume is a no-op.

<<helper functions>>=
def resume_tracking_labels(
    who: str,
    pane_id: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> None:
    """Resume tracking for a pane that regained focus.

    Reads the suspend state file and restarts the
    labels recorded there.  Deletes the file afterwards.
    """
    state_path = suspend_state_path(
        pane_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    labels = read_suspend_state(state_path)
    if not labels:
        return

    delete_suspend_state(state_path)
    start_tracking_labels(
        who=who,
        labels=labels,
        start_time=datetime.datetime.now(),
        update_tmux_context=False,
    )
@

For nested [[track]] commands inside a tracked tmux window, we must not
trust inherited environment variables such as [[TMUX_WINDOW_ID]].  A new
tmux window created with [[^b c]] would inherit the shell environment
from the old one, falsely claiming to belong to the tracked window.
Instead we derive the current [[window_id]] from the live [[TMUX_PANE]]
every time, then constrain any state lookup to the current tmux server
namespace.

<<helper functions>>=
def get_tmux_window_id_for_pane(
    pane_id: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the tmux window id containing ``pane_id``."""
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "display-message",
                "-p",
                "-t",
                pane_id,
                "#{window_id}",
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    window_id = result.stdout.strip()
    if not window_id:
        return None
    return window_id
@

Before querying tmux for the current window, we check whether any
tracked-window state files exist in the current tmux server namespace at
all.  This avoids a subprocess call to tmux in the common case where
window tracking is not in use.

<<helper functions>>=
def has_tracked_window_state(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> bool:
    """Return whether this worker currently has tracked windows."""
    pattern = (
        f"window_labels_{sanitize_tmux_id(tmux_server_key)}_*"
        f"_{sanitize_who(who)}.json"
    )
    return any(get_tmux_state_dir().glob(pattern))
@

The main entry point for nested commands combines the guards above: it
resolves the live window from [[TMUX_PANE]], checks for existing
tracked-window state in the current tmux server, and only returns a
[[window_id]] when the window is already tracked by nytid.

<<helper functions>>=
def get_current_tracked_window_id(
    who: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the live tmux window id if it is already tracked."""
    pane_id = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if not pane_id:
        return None
    if not has_tracked_window_state(
        who, tmux_server_key,
    ):
        return None
    window_id = get_tmux_window_id_for_pane(pane_id)
    if not window_id:
        return None
    if read_window_labels(
        window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    ) is None:
        return None
    return window_id
@

Nested launches that want to branch off from their current tracked
context need a slightly higher-level helper than
[[get_current_tracked_window_id()]].  The command cares about the labels
that define the current pane or window, not just its tmux identifier.

We prefer tracked-window labels over tracked-pane labels because window
tracking is the broader context: a nested command in a tracked window
should inherit that window's work labels even if no pane-specific state
exists.  Only when no tracked window owns the current pane do we fall
back to pane labels.  Outside tracked tmux state, the helper returns an
empty list so launch commands can treat inheritance as a no-op.

<<helper functions>>=
def get_current_tracked_context_labels(
    who: str,
) -> list[str]:
    """Return labels from the current tracked tmux context."""
    tmux_server_key = get_current_tmux_server_key()
    if tmux_server_key is None:
        return []

    window_id = get_current_tracked_window_id(
        who, tmux_server_key,
    )
    if window_id:
        return read_window_labels(
            window_id, who, tmux_server_key,
        ) or []

    pane_id = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if not pane_id:
        return []
    return read_pane_labels(
        pane_id, who, tmux_server_key,
    ) or []
@

\subsubsection{Hidden Detached Commands}

The focus hooks run inside tmux's [[run-shell]], which launches a
fresh Python process.  We therefore provide hidden CLI commands that
can be called safely from hooks.  Pane tracking still uses the direct
[[_suspend-detached]]/[[_resume-detached]] pair, while window tracking
uses one authoritative [[_window-switch-detached]] command driven by
[[session-window-changed]].

Window exit cleanup is a separate hidden command.  It cannot derive the
window from a live [[TMUX_PANE]] because the pane may already be dead
when the exit hook runs.  We therefore pass the explicit [[window_id]]
and remove only that pane's contributed label names from the shared
window state.

This separation matters because terminal blur alone does not prove that
the user changed tmux windows.  Pane focus hooks track terminal
attention, but window transitions are synchronized only from tmux's
selected window state.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
pane_id_opt = typer.Option(
    "--pane-id",
    help="Tmux pane ID (internal use)",
)

window_id_opt = typer.Option(
    "--window-id",
    help="Tmux window ID (internal use)",
)

tmux_server_key_opt = typer.Option(
    "--server-key",
    help="Tmux server key (internal use)",
)

session_id_opt = typer.Option(
    "--session-id",
    help="Tmux session ID (internal use)",
)
@

<<detached suspend command>>=
@cli.command(
    name="_suspend-detached", hidden=True
)
def suspend_detached(
    pane_id: Annotated[str, pane_id_opt],
    tmux_server_key: Annotated[str, tmux_server_key_opt],
    who: Annotated[str, who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """Suspend labels when a tracked pane loses focus."""
    labels = with_tmux_context_lock(
        lambda: read_pane_labels(
            pane_id, who, tmux_server_key,
        )
    )
    if not labels:
        return
    suspend_tracking_labels(
        who=who,
        labels=labels,
        pane_id=pane_id,
        tmux_server_key=tmux_server_key,
    )
@

The session hook is now authoritative.  It observes the selected window,
compares it with [[last_selected_window]], snapshots the labels attached
to the source and target windows under [[with_tmux_context_lock]], and
then executes the corresponding stop/start transition from that captured
state.

<<helper functions>>=
def get_tmux_selected_window_id(
    session_id: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the currently selected tmux window for ``session_id``."""
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "display-message",
                "-p",
                "-t",
                session_id,
                "#{window_id}",
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    window_id = result.stdout.strip()
    if not window_id:
        return None
    return window_id
@

The pane-exit cleanup needs to tell two situations apart: a window whose
last pane has just died (the file should go) versus a window that still
has other live panes (the file must stay, even with no labels left).  A
reference count cannot distinguish them, so we ask tmux directly.
Querying the [[window_id]] target succeeds only while the window still
exists; tmux exits non-zero once it is gone, which [[check=True]] turns
into the [[CalledProcessError]] we map to [[False]].  We accept
[[tmux_server_key]] for symmetry with the other window helpers even
though [[display-message]] resolves the id within the ambient server.

<<helper functions>>=
def tmux_window_exists(
    window_id: str,
    tmux_server_key: str,
) -> bool:
    """Return whether ``window_id`` still exists in tmux."""
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [
                "tmux",
                "display-message",
                "-p",
                "-t",
                window_id,
                "#{window_id}",
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return False
    return bool(result.stdout.strip())
@

Because tmux's [[$0:!]] target is unreliable in [[run-shell]]
subprocesses (see the [[last_selected_window_path]] discussion above),
the switch command reads the source window from our self-managed state
file instead.  It then writes the newly selected window for the next
invocation while still holding the tmux-context lock.

When [[tmux new-window]] creates a tracked window, the parent's
launch/setup phase (see~[[<<launch tmux command and install hooks>>]])
has already updated [[last_selected_window]] before the detached hook can
run.  A redundant hook therefore reads back the same source and target
window ID, and [[prev == selected]] discards it immediately.

An absent selected window ID is another case where the hook must do
nothing.  [[get_tmux_selected_window_id()]] returns [[None]] when the
detached subprocess cannot read tmux state reliably---for example if the
session has already disappeared.  Treating that observation failure as a
real switch to an untracked window would spuriously stop the source
labels, so we bail out before mutating tracking state.

The target side reads the window's per-label \emph{batch ids}, not
just its labels, and resumes through [[restart_window_batches]] so the
window's batch structure survives the suspend/resume cycle.  The
source side needs no such care: stopping an explicit label list
already records one history entry per batch, and the source window's
file keeps its ids as the grouping key for the next switch back.

<<detached window switch command>>=
@cli.command(
    name="_window-switch-detached", hidden=True
)
def window_switch_detached(
    tmux_server_key: Annotated[str, tmux_server_key_opt],
    session_id: Annotated[str, session_id_opt],
    who: Annotated[str, who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """Synchronize tracking to tmux's selected window."""
    selected_window_id = get_tmux_selected_window_id(
        session_id,
    )
    if selected_window_id is None:
        return

    def read_and_update():
        prev_window_id = read_last_selected_window(
            who, tmux_server_key, session_id,
        )
        if (
            prev_window_id
            and prev_window_id == selected_window_id
        ):
            return None
        source_labels = None
        if prev_window_id is not None:
            source_labels = read_window_labels(
                prev_window_id,
                who,
                tmux_server_key,
            )
        target_batches = read_window_label_batches(
            selected_window_id,
            who,
            tmux_server_key,
        )
        write_last_selected_window(
            who,
            tmux_server_key,
            session_id,
            selected_window_id,
        )
        return (
            prev_window_id,
            source_labels,
            target_batches,
        )

    result = with_tmux_context_lock(read_and_update)
    if result is None:
        return
    (
        prev_window_id,
        source_labels,
        target_batches,
    ) = result
    transition_time = datetime.datetime.now()
    if source_labels:
        stop_tracking_labels(
            who=who,
            labels=source_labels,
            end_time=transition_time,
            quiet_if_missing=True,
            update_tmux_context=False,
        )
    if target_batches:
        restart_window_batches(
            who=who,
            window_id=selected_window_id,
            tmux_server_key=tmux_server_key,
            label_batches=target_batches,
            start_time=transition_time,
        )
    emit_window_event_debug(
        who,
        tmux_server_key,
        session_id,
        "execute-switch",
        (
            f"source={prev_window_id or '(none)'} "
            f"target={selected_window_id}"
        ),
        summarize_window_transition(
            prev_window_id,
            source_labels,
            selected_window_id,
            list(target_batches or {}),
        ),
    )
@

<<detached resume command>>=
