\subsubsection{Migrating and Cleaning State Files}
\label{track:server-sweep}

Two kinds of state files can end up unaddressable by the current
code: files written by an older nytid---under the persistent
tracking directory (before \cref{track:tmux-state-dir}) or under a
key computed from the unreliable [[$TMUX]] PID field (before
\cref{track:server-key})---and files left behind by a server that
was killed and restarted without a reboot, which fires no
[[pane-exited]] hooks and does not clear the runtime directory.

An earlier design \emph{deleted} the second kind at every tracked
launch, reasoning that a file whose key token shares the current
sockethash but differs in generation must belong to a dead server:
a socket hosts at most one live server at a time.  That inference is
subtly wrong, and expensively so.  The generation was computed from
the [[$TMUX]] PID field, which different clients of the same live
server disagree about (\cref{track:server-key})---so the
\enquote{provably dead} files being deleted were the \emph{live}
server's own state, written moments earlier under its other alias.
One launch from the wrong terminal was enough to destroy the
tracked status of every open window.  The lesson we draw from that
failure is a rule: \emph{never delete state on an inference}.  What
runs automatically now only \emph{renames} files, and actual
removal happens solely in the explicit [[nytid track clean]],
guarded by what the live server itself reports.

Routine accumulation no longer needs any cleanup at all: dead
generations from server restarts sit inert---their key never
matches---until the next reboot clears the runtime directory
(\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}).

Every state filename carries the server key as one hyphen-joined
token, so files can be classified from their names alone.  A file
keyed to a \emph{different} sockethash may belong to a live server
we simply are not attached to (a [[tmux -L work]] side server, or
another host sharing this home directory over NFS), so neither
migration nor [[clean]] may touch it.  Two legacy name formats,
finally, can no longer be produced: files from before the server
key existed (their token is the literal [[window]] or [[pane]] from
the sanitized tmux identifier, or empty for old last-window files),
and files whose token is a bare generation-less sockethash.  The
[[window_events_]] prefix covers debug state files from a since
removed design.

<<constants>>=
TMUX_STATE_FILE_PREFIXES = (
    "window_labels_",
    "pane_labels_",
    "suspend_",
    "last_window_",
    "window_events_",
)
LEGACY_STATE_KEY_TOKENS = frozenset({"window", "pane", ""})
@

Recovering the key token from a filename relies on the key being the
first underscore-free token after the prefix, which
[[sanitize_tmux_id]] guarantees for current keys and which happens to
classify the legacy formats correctly as well.

<<helper functions>>=
def parse_state_file_server_key(
    filename: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the server-key token of a tmux state filename.

    Returns ``None`` for filenames that are no tmux state files.
    """
    for prefix in TMUX_STATE_FILE_PREFIXES:
        if filename.startswith(prefix):
            rest = filename[len(prefix):]
            return rest.split("_", 1)[0]
    return None
@

Both migration and [[clean]] ask the live server what actually
exists, in one [[list-panes -a]] call that yields every window and
pane id.  A failed listing returns [[None]] rather than empty sets:
the callers must be able to distinguish \enquote{the server has no
windows} from \enquote{we could not ask}, because acting on the
latter as if it were the former is precisely the
delete-on-inference mistake this design retires.  The same
motivation puts liveness above [[tmux_window_exists]]: that helper
maps a failed query to [[False]], which is fine for its hook-side
callers but would count as \enquote{dead} here.

<<helper functions>>=
def list_live_tmux_ids() -> tuple[set[str], set[str]] | None:
    """Return the live server's window and pane id sets.

    Returns ``None`` when the server cannot be listed.
    """
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a",
             "-F", "#{window_id} #{pane_id}"],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, OSError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(result.stdout, str):
        return None
    windows: set[str] = set()
    panes: set[str] = set()
    for line in result.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) == 2:
            windows.add(parts[0])
            panes.add(parts[1])
    return windows, panes
@

To match a state file against that listing we need the window or
pane id embedded in its name.  The ids are numeric, which is what
lets one regular expression find them even in the legacy key-less
formats: a key token never puts an underscore directly after a run
of digits, so the pattern cannot misfire on the key itself.

<<helper functions>>=
_STATE_FILE_ID_RE = re.compile(r"_(window|pane)_(\d+)_")

def parse_state_file_tmux_id(
    filename: str,
) -> str | None:
    """Return the tmux window/pane id named in a state filename."""
    match = _STATE_FILE_ID_RE.search(filename)
    if not match:
        return None
    sigil = "@" if match.group(1) == "window" else "%"
    return sigil + match.group(2)
@

State files may sit in two places: the runtime directory, and the
persistent tracking directory where older nytid versions kept them.
The test fixtures point both helpers at one directory, so we
deduplicate rather than assume they differ.

<<helper functions>>=
def _tmux_state_dirs() -> list[pathlib.Path]:
    """Return the directories that may hold tmux state files."""
    dirs = [get_tmux_state_dir()]
    tracking_dir = get_tracking_dir()
    if tracking_dir != dirs[0]:
        dirs.append(tracking_dir)
    return dirs
