Those three tests are the only place in the suite where a [[track]]
test writes the persistent configuration, and they write it for real:
there is no patched [[config]] object here, only [[typerconf]] reading
and rewriting a file on disk.  Which file that is decides whether the
suite is safe to run on a machine somebody works on, and it is settled
somewhere else entirely---in the sandbox that [[conftest.py]] installs
before it imports anything from [[nytid]] (\cref{conftest}).

An arrangement that spans two files and only matters when it breaks is
the kind that a later refactoring can undo without anyone noticing,
because nothing above fails when it does: setting [[track.debug]] and
reading it back succeeds just as well against the user's own
configuration.  So we check the other half of the claim---that the real
file did not move---while making sure the write we are checking really
happened.

The [[preserve_config]] fixture, which the tests above use to hand the
next test the configuration they found, gives us the sandbox path to
compare against; [[real_config_fingerprint]] gives us the path the
sandbox replaced (\cref{sec:config-fixtures}).

