\subsection{Add Command}

The [[add]] command lets users record time spent away from the computer:
meetings at the whiteboard, phone calls, travel.  The entry is complete
(it has both a start and an end time) from the moment it is created, so
it goes straight to the history file without touching the active session.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
add_labels_arg = typer.Argument(
    ..., help="Labels for the time entry",
    autocompletion=complete_historical_labels)
duration_opt = typer.Option(
    "--duration", "-d",
    help="Duration (hours unless suffixed, "
         "e.g. 1.5, 1.5h, 90m, 1h30m)")
add_description_opt = typer.Option(
    "--description",
    help="Description for this time entry")
@

The original implementation had a [[--start-offset]] option that accepted
an integer (minutes only).  This was inconsistent with the string-based
[[--offset]] option on [[track start]] which accepts suffixes like
\enquote{30m} or \enquote{1.5h}.  We replace [[--start-offset]] with the
same [[--offset]] and [[--at]] pair used by [[track start]], so users need
to learn only one set of time-specification options.

<<add command>>=
@cli.command()
def add(
    labels: Annotated[list[str], add_labels_arg],
    duration: Annotated[str, duration_opt],
    offset: Annotated[str | None,
                      offset_opt] = None,
    at: Annotated[str | None, at_opt] = None,
    description: Annotated[str,
                           add_description_opt] = "",
    who: Annotated[str, who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """
    Add a completed time entry for work that already happened.

    Use this for meetings, hallway conversations, or whiteboard sessions that
    were never started in the tracker while they were happening.

    \b
    Examples:
      nytid track add meeting --duration 1
      nytid track add DD1310 supervision --duration 45m --offset -90m
      nytid track add admin --duration 30m --at 14:00 --description "Timesheet review"
    """
    if not labels:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: At least one label is required",
            err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    hours = parse_hours(duration)
    if hours <= 0:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: Duration must be positive",
            err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    <<resolve add start time>>

    end_time = start_time + datetime.timedelta(
        hours=hours
    )

    entry = TrackingEntry(
        start_time, end_time, labels, description,
        who=who
    )
    add_completed_entries([entry])

    typer.echo(
        f"Added entry: "
        f"{get_labels_display(labels)} "
        f"({format_duration(entry.duration())})"
    )
    if description:
        typer.echo(f"Description: {description}")
    typer.echo(
        f"Time: {start_time.strftime('%H:%M')} - "
        f"{end_time.strftime('%H:%M')}"
    )
@

[[--offset]] and [[--at]] are mutually exclusive for the same reason as
in [[track start]]: they address the same question (when did this session
start?) from two different angles, and combining them would be ambiguous.

<<resolve add start time>>=
if offset is not None and at is not None:
    typer.echo(
        "Error: Cannot use both --offset and --at",
        err=True
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

now = datetime.datetime.now()
start_time = now

if at is not None:
    try:
        start_time = parse_at_time(at)
    except ValueError as e:
        typer.echo(f"Error: {e}", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)
elif offset is not None:
    try:
        start_time += parse_offset(offset)
    except ValueError as e:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error parsing offset '{offset}': {e}",
            err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

\subsection{Shared Entry Filtering}

The analysis commands ([[ls]], [[today]], [[stats]], [[export]]) share
the same load-then-filter pattern.  When the filtering logic was inlined in each
command, adding a new filter dimension (such as the [[--who]] flag)
required updating every command in lock-step --- a maintenance hazard
that invited subtle inconsistencies.  Extracting the pipeline into a
single helper means that a new filter dimension is added once and all
commands benefit.  Each command still owns its error messages and
early-return logic, keeping presentation concerns separate from
filtering policy.

The reports add one extra step on top of this shared helper.  [[ls]]
and [[export]] interpret [[--days]] as a rolling age limit, but
[[today]] and [[stats]] present named calendar dates.  They therefore
narrow the already-filtered entries to the requested date range with
[[clip_entries_to_dates()]] (\cref{sec:track-calendar-periods}) so the
summary and the table agree even around midnight.

<<helper functions>>=
def filter_entries(
    days=None, who=None, labels=None
):
    """Load and filter tracking entries.

    Returns filtered list, or None if no data file
    exists or it is empty.
    """
    entries = load_tracking_data()
    if not entries:
        return None

    if days is not None:
        cutoff = (
            datetime.datetime.now()
            - datetime.timedelta(days=days)
        )
        entries = [
            e for e in entries
            if e.start_time >= cutoff
        ]

    if who is not None:
        entries = [
            e for e in entries
            if e.who == who
        ]

    if labels:
        entries = [
            e for e in entries
            if any(label in e.labels for label in labels)
        ]

    return entries
@

We unit-test the shared [[filter_entries]] helper directly to confirm
each filter dimension works independently:

<<test functions>>=
def test_filter_entries(temp_tracking_dir):
    """Test the shared filter_entries helper."""
    runner.invoke(cli, [
        "add", "DD1310", "grading",
        "--duration", "1",
        "--who", "alice"
    ])
    runner.invoke(cli, [
        "add", "DD1321", "lecture",
        "--duration", "45m",
        "--who", "bob"
    ])

    # No filters --- returns all
    all_entries = filter_entries()
    assert len(all_entries) == 2

    # Filter by worker
    alice = filter_entries(who="alice")
    assert len(alice) == 1
    assert alice[0].who == "alice"

    # Filter by label
    grading = filter_entries(labels=["grading"])
    assert len(grading) == 1
    assert "grading" in grading[0].labels

    # Filter by days (all recent)
    recent = filter_entries(days=1)
    assert len(recent) == 2

    # Combined filters
    result = filter_entries(
        who="alice", labels=["DD1310"]
    )
    assert len(result) == 1

def test_merge_duplicate_tracking_entries_warns(caplog):
    """Exact duplicate history rows are collapsed with a warning."""
    entry = TrackingEntry(
        datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 10, 0, 0),
        datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 11, 0, 0),
        ["lecture"],
        "Weekly sync",
        who="dbosk",
    )

    with caplog.at_level(logging.WARNING):
        merged = merge_duplicate_tracking_entries([entry, entry])

    assert len(merged) == 1
    assert "Merged 1 duplicate tracking entry" in caplog.text

def test_normalize_tracking_entries_reports_duplicate_count(caplog):
    """Normalization reports how many duplicates were removed."""
    entry = TrackingEntry(
        datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 10, 0, 0),
        datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 11, 0, 0),
        ["lecture"],
        who="dbosk",
    )

    with caplog.at_level(logging.WARNING):
        merged, duplicate_count = normalize_tracking_entries([entry, entry])

    assert len(merged) == 1
    assert duplicate_count == 1
    assert "Merged 1 duplicate tracking entry" in caplog.text
@

The tuple key inside [[normalize_tracking_entries]] is only correct if
it partitions entries exactly like [[content_hash()]] does.  We pin
that equivalence with a mixed batch---duplicates, near-duplicates
differing in a single field, and label lists given in different
orders---and check that tuple-based dedup keeps precisely the entries
that digest-based dedup would keep:

<<test functions>>=
def test_normalize_dedup_matches_content_hash():
    """Tuple-key dedup selects the same survivors as hash dedup."""
    start = datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 10, 0, 0)
    end = datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 12, 11, 0, 0)
    entries = [
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["DD1310", "lecture"], "prep"),
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["lecture", "DD1310"], "prep"),
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["DD1310", "lecture"], "grading"),
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["DD1310"], "prep"),
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["DD1310"], "prep", who="alice"),
        TrackingEntry(start, end, ["DD1310"], "prep", who="alice"),
    ]

    merged, duplicate_count = normalize_tracking_entries(entries)

    expected = []
    seen_hashes = set()
    for entry in entries:
        entry_hash = entry.content_hash()
        if entry_hash in seen_hashes:
            continue
        seen_hashes.add(entry_hash)
        expected.append(entry)

    assert merged == expected
    assert duplicate_count == len(entries) - len(expected)
@

\subsection{Listing Tracked Sessions}
\label{sec:track-ls}

The [[ls]] command fills a gap between [[status]] (which shows only the
\emph{currently active} session) and [[stats]] (which shows
\emph{aggregated} totals).  Sometimes you just want to see the last few
individual entries --- for example, to verify that yesterday's grading
session was recorded, or to eyeball recent work before writing a
timesheet.  The [[ls]] command provides this quick, per-entry view.

Unlike [[stats]], which defaults to a 1-day time window, [[ls]] uses a
count limit ([[-n]]) instead.  A time window would hide old entries even
when the user explicitly asks \enquote{show me my last 20 sessions}.
The count-based approach always returns exactly as many entries as
requested (or fewer if the history is shorter).

When the user wants to repair history, though, a bare timestamp line is
not enough: they need a stable way to name one specific entry.  The
[[--id]] flag, on by default, prepends a short, unique prefix of each
entry's [[content_hash()]], giving us Git-style identification without
storing a separate persistent identifier.

Both the default row count and the default for the id column are worth
configuring, since users tend to settle on a personal preference.  We
read them once at module import through the shared [[default]] helper
(\cref{cli.utils.defaults}).
<<constants>>=
TRACK_LS_COUNT_CONFIG = "track.ls.count"
TRACK_LS_ID_CONFIG = "track.ls.id"

DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_COUNT = default(TRACK_LS_COUNT_CONFIG, 10, int)
DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_ID = default(TRACK_LS_ID_CONFIG, True, bool)
@

The sort key joins that same category.  The question a user normally
asks at the prompt is \enquote{what did I most recently finish?} ---
not \enquote{what did I most recently start?}.  The two questions
diverge whenever sessions overlap or get backfilled with [[--at]]: a
long morning block and a brief mid-morning one have opposite orderings
under the two keys, and only the stop-time view puts the
finished-most-recently session at the top where a glance expects it.
So [[stop]] is the default, and [[start]] remains available for users
who prefer the legacy view (e.g.\ when spotting sessions logged in the
wrong order).

We encode the two valid choices in a [[str]]-backed [[Enum]] so Typer
renders them in [[--help]] and supplies shell completion for free, the
same pattern used by [[ExportFormat]] later in this module.  The
fallback in [[default()]] is the [[Enum]] member itself, while the
[[cast]] is the [[Enum]] class --- calling [[TrackLsSort("stop")]]
constructs the member from the stored config value, and an
unrecognised string raises [[ValueError]] which [[default()]] catches
and replaces with the fallback.

<<constants>>=
class TrackLsSort(str, Enum):
    start = "start"
    stop = "stop"

TRACK_LS_SORT_CONFIG = "track.ls.sort"
DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_SORT = default(
    TRACK_LS_SORT_CONFIG,
    TrackLsSort.stop,
    TrackLsSort,
)
@

<<argument and option definitions>>=
ls_labels_arg = typer.Argument(
    help="Filter by labels "
         "(shows all if not specified)",
    autocompletion=complete_historical_labels)
ls_number_opt = typer.Option(
    "--number", "-n",
    help="Number of entries to show. "
         "[config: track.ls.count]")
ls_id_opt = typer.Option(
    "--id/--no-id",
    help="Column with short content-hash prefixes "
         "identifying entries (on by default). "
         "[config: track.ls.id]")
ls_sort_opt = typer.Option(
    "--sort",
    help="Sort entries by start or stop time "
         "(most recent first). "
         "[config: track.ls.sort]")
@

<<ls command>>=
@cli.command(name="ls")
def ls_entries(
    labels: Annotated[list[str],
                      ls_labels_arg] = None,
    n: Annotated[int, ls_number_opt] = DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_COUNT,
    show_id: Annotated[bool, ls_id_opt] = DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_ID,
    sort_by: Annotated[TrackLsSort,
                       ls_sort_opt] = DEFAULT_TRACK_LS_SORT,
    who: Annotated[str | None,
                   who_filter_opt] = None,
):
    """
    List recent completed tracking entries.

    Use this to review the last few sessions, filter by course or worker, or
    show short IDs before editing or removing an entry.

    \b
    Examples:
      nytid track ls
      nytid track ls DD1310 -n 5
      nytid track ls --id
      nytid track ls --sort start
      nytid track ls --who Dan-Claude
    """
    <<load and filter for ls>>
    <<display ls entries>>
@

We use the shared [[filter_entries]] helper but skip the time-window
filter --- the count limit ([[-n]]) controls how many entries appear
instead:

<<load and filter for ls>>=
entries = filter_entries(who=who, labels=labels)
if not entries:
    typer.echo("No tracking data available.")
    return
@

Entries are sorted most-recent-first because the typical use case is
checking recent work --- older entries scroll off the terminal rather
than consuming attention.  The [[sort_by]] argument picks which
timestamp drives that ordering: [[stop]] (the default, for reasons
given when we introduced [[TrackLsSort]]) and [[start]] (the legacy
view).  The selector lives inside the [[key]] lambda so we keep a
single [[entries.sort()]] call rather than duplicating the call
across an [[if]]/[[else]] on [[sort_by]] --- the two key choices
stay in one place where a future third key (say, [[duration]])
can join them.  Each entry shows start--end times so the reader
does not have to mentally add start plus duration.

Everything after the sort is a listing like every other one in nytid,
so we hand it to [[print_rows]] (\cref{cli.utils.output}) rather than
decide here what a terminal and a pipe should each receive.  This
module imports the helper's two names directly instead of the module,
because [[export]] below has a parameter called [[output]] and a
module-level binding of that name would be shadowed inside it; the
import itself sits in the module chunk of \cref{sec:track-code-outline}
with all the others, which is where this file keeps them.

The id prefixes are the only consumer of [[content_hash()]] in this
listing, so we compute them only when the id column is requested:
with [[--no-id]] the command never hashes at all, which matters once
the history holds thousands of entries.

When the column \emph{is} requested, the set we abbreviate against
has to be the \emph{whole history}, not the rows on screen.  An id is
only useful because [[show]], [[edit]] and [[rm]] accept it, and
those commands resolve it against everything
[[load_tracking_data]] returns (\cref{track:history-helpers}).  A
prefix unique among the rows we print need not be unique there: two
entries can share their first six hex digits and differ only in
[[who]], in which case [[--who]] hides one of them from the listing
while [[rm]] still sees both and refuses the id we just printed.
Hashing the whole history closes that gap---the printed id is by
construction unique in the set it will be resolved against.  This is
the same rule [[complete_entry_hashes]] follows for shell
completion, and for the same reason.

The rows we print stay the filtered, sliced ones; only the
\emph{abbreviation set} widens.  When no filter is in effect the
listing already covers the whole history, so we reuse the entries we
have rather than reading the history file a second time.  That
shortcut restates, at the call site, the condition under which
[[filter_entries]] returns its input untouched---if a third filter
dimension is ever added to the [[ls]] call, this test has to learn
about it too, or the abbreviation set silently narrows again.

<<display ls entries>>=
entries.sort(
    key=lambda e: (
        e.end_time
        if sort_by == TrackLsSort.stop
        else e.start_time
    ),
    reverse=True,
)
if show_id:
    history = (
        entries
        if who is None and not labels
        else load_tracking_data()
    )
    hash_prefixes = abbreviate_hashes(
        [entry.content_hash() for entry in history]
    )
else:
    hash_prefixes = {}
<<collect the ls rows>>
<<print the ls listing>>
@

A row is a tuple where it used to be a dictionary.  The keys existed to
let two hand-written renderers pick fields out by name; with one
renderer that consumes positional tuples, the column order is stated
once---in the column list below---and the rows simply follow it.
Keeping the dictionary would mean maintaining a mapping whose only
remaining reader is the loop that immediately flattens it again.

The optional id is \emph{prepended} to the finished tuple rather than
threaded through its construction as a leading [[None]].  That lets the
same [[if show_id]] shape appear here and in the column list, so the two
decisions that have to agree---is there an id field, is there an id
column---are written the same way and are awkward to change apart.

The prefix lookup is a [[get]] rather than an index because the
filtered branch above abbreviates a \emph{second} read of the history
file: a [[track stop]] landing between the two reads leaves a
displayed entry out of the map, and a listing that dies with a
[[KeyError]] traceback serves the reader worse than one that prints
the plain minimum-length prefix and lets [[resolve_tracking_entry]]
explain any resulting ambiguity in words.

<<collect the ls rows>>=
rows = []
for entry in entries[:n]:
    ts_start = entry.start_time.strftime(
        "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"
    )
    ts_end = entry.end_time.strftime("%H:%M")
    row = (
        f"{ts_start}\u2013{ts_end}",
        format_duration(entry.duration()),
        get_labels_display(entry.labels),
        entry.who,
        entry.description or "",
    )
    if show_id:
        full_hash = entry.content_hash()
        row = (
            hash_prefixes.get(
                full_hash,
                full_hash[
                    :TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
                ],
            ),
        ) + row
    rows.append(row)
@

The column specifications are the ones the hand-written Rich table
already used, transcribed into [[Column]] records, so the table a
terminal user sees is unchanged.  They split the fields in two.
Timestamps, durations, ids and usernames have a width their own format
fixes, so they are [[no_wrap]]: folding a sixteen-character timestamp
onto a second line would spend a screen line to reveal nothing.  Labels
and descriptions are the free-text fields and therefore [[fold]] ---
truncating a description removes exactly the part the reader has not
read yet, whereas folding it costs another line.

<<print the ls listing>>=
columns = [
    Column("Time", style="cyan", no_wrap=True),
    Column("Duration", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
    Column("Labels", fold=True),
    Column("Who", no_wrap=True),
    Column("Description", fold=True),
]
if show_id:
    columns.insert(
        0, Column("ID", style="dim", no_wrap=True)
    )
print_rows(columns, rows)
@

\subsubsection{What changes for a pipe}
\label{sec:track-piped-listings}

The piped form is the part that genuinely changes.  It used to be one
hand-rolled line per entry, joined with two spaces and reshaped on the
way out: the worker was wrapped in brackets, and a description, when
there was one, was introduced by a dash, so an entry read
\begin{quote}
[[2026-03-12 10:00–11:00  1h 0m 0s  DD1310  [dbosk]  - Graded homework]]
\end{quote}
It is now those same values in the column order above with a single
tab between each, and nothing else added: no brackets, no dash, no
alignment.

Three of those differences matter to a script rather than to a reader.
The brackets and the dash were decoration that a consumer had to strip
back off before it had the value.  The two-space join could not be
split on reliably: several fields carry single spaces of their own ---
the timestamp, [[DD1310, grading]], any description --- so ordinary
whitespace splitting tears them apart, while splitting on exactly two
spaces breaks the first time a description contains a double space.  A
tab cannot occur inside any of these values at all.  And an entry
without a description now ends in an \emph{empty} field rather than
stopping a field early, which is what makes [[cut -f4]] mean
\enquote{the worker} on every line instead of only on the lines that
happened to carry a description.

The one positional subtlety left is the id column, which shifts every
other field by one when it is present.  Because [[track.ls.id]]
defaults to true and is user-configurable, a script should pass
[[--id]] or [[--no-id]] explicitly rather than inherit whichever
setting the user happens to have.

Changing these bytes is safe in a way it would not have been for, say,
[[courses mine ls]].  Nothing we ship reads them: the cron wrapper
[[nytid.hourly.sh]] drives [[courses]], [[hr]], [[schedule]] and
[[todo next]], and never [[track ls]] or [[track stats]].  What we buy
for that is a single CLI-wide contract --- a labelled, aligned table
for a person and stable headerless \textsc{tsv} for a pipe --- which
now holds for the [[courses]], [[hr]], [[schedule]] and [[todo]]
listings too.

Two neighbouring behaviours are deliberately untouched.  The
\enquote{No tracking data available.} message still comes from
[[<<load and filter for ls>>]], before any rendering happens:
[[print_rows]]'s silence on an empty listing is about not emitting rows
that do not exist, not about refusing to tell a person why their screen
is blank.  And the hidden detached commands, which print nothing under
any circumstances, do not route through here at all --- their silence
is still the one [[test_stop_detached_quiet_when_missing]] pins.

Let us hold the new bytes to that description exactly, since exactness
is the whole point of the piped form:

<<test functions>>=
def test_ls_pipes_headerless_tsv(temp_tracking_dir):
    """Piped ls rows are tab-separated and undecorated."""
    runner.invoke(cli, [
        "add", "DD1310", "grading",
        "--duration", "1",
        "--at", "2026-03-12 10:00",
        "--description", "Graded homework"
    ])

    result = runner.invoke(cli, ["ls", "--no-id", "-n", "1"])

    assert result.exit_code == 0
    assert result.stdout == (
        "2026-03-12 10:00\u201311:00\t1h 0m 0s\t"
        f"DD1310, grading\t{default_username}\t"
        "Graded homework\n"
    )
@

The field \emph{positions} are the other half of the contract, and they
are what the id column and an absent description perturb.  One entry
lacking a description, listed both ways, pins both at once: the same
five fields appear in both listings, the last of them empty, and
[[--id]] adds a sixth in front without disturbing the rest.

<<test functions>>=
def test_ls_tsv_field_positions_are_stable(temp_tracking_dir):
    """--id prepends a field; a missing description leaves one empty."""
    runner.invoke(cli, [
        "add", "DD1310",
        "--duration", "1",
        "--at", "2026-03-12 10:00"
    ])

    plain = runner.invoke(cli, ["ls", "--no-id", "-n", "1"])
    with_id = runner.invoke(cli, ["ls", "--id", "-n", "1"])

    assert plain.exit_code == 0
    assert with_id.exit_code == 0
    plain_fields = plain.stdout.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
    id_fields = with_id.stdout.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
    assert len(plain_fields) == 5
    assert plain_fields[-1] == ""
    assert len(id_fields) == 6
    assert id_fields[1:] == plain_fields
    assert len(id_fields[0]) >= TRACKING_HASH_PREFIX_MIN_LENGTH
@

The same hash prefixes become the entry point for precise history
inspection and repair.  [[show]] prints one historical entry in full,
[[edit]] replaces one entry with updated content, and [[rm]] removes one
entry after confirmation.  All three resolve a hash prefix against the
current history so unrelated additions do not shift the user's target.

For [[edit]] alone, the hash is optional.  A hash \emph{cannot} name
the session that is still running: [[content_hash]] digests the end
time, which an in-progress session does not have yet.  So a hashless
[[edit]] is unambiguous, and we give it the natural reading: edit
\enquote{what I am tracking right now}---the same most-recent batch a
bare [[stop]] would end.  The scenario is starting a session, noticing
the labels are wrong, and correcting them without the collateral
damage of a stop-and-restart.  \Cref{sec:track-edit-active} works out
that mode; the historical mode follows first.

<<history entry helpers>>=
def parse_edit_tracking_start_time(
    at: str | None,
    original: TrackingEntry,
) -> datetime.datetime:
    """Return the edited start time for a historical entry."""
    if at is None:
        return original.start_time
    return parse_at_time(at, reference_time=original.start_time)

def parse_edit_tracking_end_time(
    end_at: str | None,
    duration_hours: float | None,
    start_time: datetime.datetime,
    original: TrackingEntry,
) -> datetime.datetime:
    """Return the edited end time for a historical entry."""
    if end_at is not None and duration_hours is not None:
        raise ValueError(
            "Cannot use both --end-at and --duration"
        )
    if end_at is not None:
        return parse_at_time(
            end_at,
            reference_time=original.end_time,
        )
    if duration_hours is not None:
        if duration_hours <= 0:
            raise ValueError("Duration must be positive")
        return start_time + datetime.timedelta(
            hours=duration_hours
        )
    return original.end_time

def render_tracking_entry(entry: TrackingEntry) -> list[str]:
    """Render one historical entry for human inspection."""
    return [
        f"ID: {entry.content_hash()}",
        f"Start: {entry.start_time.isoformat(sep=' ')}",
        f"End: {entry.end_time.isoformat(sep=' ')}",
        f"Duration: {format_duration(entry.duration())}",
        f"Labels: {get_labels_display(entry.labels)}",
        f"Who: {entry.who}",
        f"Description: {entry.description}" if entry.description else "Description:",
    ]
@

The [[show]], [[edit]], and [[rm]] commands all identify one entry by the
short hash prefix produced by [[track ls --id]].  Completion needs the
same abbreviation rule as the list view so the shell offers prefixes the
commands can actually resolve.  We therefore derive completion candidates
from [[abbreviate_hashes]] rather than guessing a fixed prefix length.

Opaque hashes are hard to tell apart at the prompt, so the completion
helper returns Click's annotated [[(value, help)]] form.  The inserted
value stays a short unique prefix, while the help text reuses the same
time and label formatting as [[ls]] so the user can recognize the right
entry before accepting it.

<<helper functions>>=
def complete_entry_hashes(ctx, args, incomplete):
    """
    Shell completion function for tracking entry hashes.

    Returns abbreviated entry-hash prefixes, annotated with
    a human-readable summary, for historical entries whose
    full content hash matches the incomplete prefix.
    """
    try:
        entries = load_tracking_data()
        full_hashes = [entry.content_hash() for entry in entries]
        abbreviations = abbreviate_hashes(full_hashes)
        candidates = []

        for entry, full_hash in zip(entries, full_hashes):
            if not full_hash.startswith(incomplete):
                continue

            prefix = full_hash[:max(
                len(abbreviations[full_hash]),
                len(incomplete),
            )]
            labels = get_labels_display(entry.labels)
            summary = (
                f"{entry.start_time:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M}"
                f"\u2013{entry.end_time:%H:%M}  "
                f"{labels}  [{entry.who}]"
            )
            candidates.append((prefix, summary))

        return candidates
    except:
        return []
@

We define the argument once so the help text and completion behavior stay
consistent across all three commands:

<<argument and option definitions>>=
entry_hash_arg = typer.Argument(
    ..., help="Tracking entry hash prefix",
    autocompletion=complete_entry_hashes)
@

<<show command>>=
@cli.command()
def show(
    entry_hash: Annotated[str, entry_hash_arg],
):
    """
    Show one historical tracking entry in full detail.

    Use this after `track ls --id` when you want to inspect timestamps,
    labels, worker, and description before editing or removing an entry.

    \b
    Example:
      nytid track show a1b2c3
    """
    entries = load_tracking_data()
    if not entries:
        typer.echo("No tracking data available.", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    try:
        entry = resolve_tracking_entry(entries, entry_hash)
    except ValueError as exc:
        typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    for line in render_tracking_entry(entry):
        typer.echo(line)
@

Editing uses separate option constants from [[start]] because the
semantics differ: [[start --at]] sets a new session's start time, while
[[edit --at]] overwrites the start of an existing entry.  Reusing the
same constant would conflate these two meanings in the help text.

As with [[todo edit]], plain [[--label]]/[[-l]] replaces every label,
which is clumsy when you only want to add or drop one.  We add the same
incremental [[--add-label]]/[[-a]] and [[--remove-label]]/[[-r]] flags
here, sharing the exact merge rule by importing [[apply_label_edits]] from
the \texttt{cli.utils.labels} module (\cref{cli.utils.labels}).  That
module documents the merge rule: removing first and then adding lets us
flag the same label in both lists as a contradiction rather than silently
cancelling, while absent-removals and present-adds become idempotent
stderr warnings.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
