All three label flags are known before we dispatch, and both modes
consume them, so we reject the contradictory absolute-plus-relative
combination at the very top --- there is no point loading either state
store only to discover the request was malformed:

<<reject mixing absolute and relative label edits>>=
if label is not None and (add_label or remove_label):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --label cannot be combined with "
        "--add-label/--remove-label",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

The batch selectors are meaningless with a hash---a historical entry
\emph{is} its own target, and nothing about it can be
\enquote{discarded without a history entry}---so they are rejected in
the same pre-dispatch spot:

<<reject batch options with a hash>>=
if entry_hash is not None and (containing is not None or discard):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --containing and --discard only apply "
        "without an entry hash",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Inside [[update_history]] we know the entry's [[original.labels]], so this
is where the relative merge happens.  [[apply_label_edits]] may raise
[[ValueError]] for the same-label-in-both case; that propagates out of
[[mutate_tracking_history]] and is caught by the existing error handler
below, giving a non-zero exit.  No-op warnings are stashed in the
[[label_warnings]] list declared in the enclosing scope (via [[nonlocal]])
and echoed only after the update succeeds, so a failed edit prints no
misleading warnings.  [[apply_label_edits]] returns labels in insertion
order (the surviving labels in their original order, with additions
appended), not sorted; [[TrackingEntry]] then re-sorts and de-duplicates on
construction, so the stored order is alphabetical regardless of the merge:

<<compute edited labels>>=
nonlocal label_warnings
if label is not None:
    new_labels = list(label)
elif add_label or remove_label:
    new_labels, label_warnings = apply_label_edits(
        original.labels, add_label or [], remove_label or [],
    )
else:
    new_labels = original.labels
@

\subsubsection{Editing the Active Session}
\label{sec:track-edit-active}

Without a hash, [[edit]] corrects the session that is still running.
The mode exists for one scenario: the user started tracking, realized
the labels are wrong, and wants to fix them \emph{without} collateral
damage.  Every workaround damages something.  A stop-and-restart
records a spurious entry and loses the start time; [[start --replace]]
loses the notes; a detour through [[switch]] clobbers the
[[switch_other]] toggle.  [[ActiveSession.edit_batch]] was built to do
this correction in place, and here we wire it to the command line.

The default target is the most recent batch---
[[most_recent_batch_labels]], the same selection a bare [[stop]] uses,
so the session you [[edit]] is exactly the one a following [[stop]]
would end.  [[--containing]] overrides the default with an explicit
selector, [[batch_labels_containing]]: it names the batch by one of
its labels, which is what a scripted caller needs when recency cannot
be trusted---tracking started meanwhile from another terminal steals
the default target---or when the label to fix lives in an older
batch.  Two options make
no sense here and are rejected with guidance rather than silently
ignored: [[--end-at]] and [[--duration]] describe an end time, and the
running session has none yet.  A bare [[edit]] with no options at all
most likely means a forgotten hash, so it is an error too---unlike the
historical mode, where an option-less [[edit HASH]] is a harmless
identity rewrite.

Relabeling must also reach the tmux context.  A pane started with
[[start -P wronglabel]] records that label in its pane-context file so
the pane-exit hook can stop it.  If [[edit]] renamed the label only in
the session, the hook would later stop [[wronglabel]]---a no-op---and
the corrected label would keep running unnoticed.  So
[[edit_active_batch]] syncs the current pane/window context in the
same critical section, exactly as [[start_tracking_labels]] and
[[stop_tracking_labels]] do.  Like those, it syncs the \emph{current}
context: correcting from a different pane leaves the original pane's
file stale, the same asymmetry a [[stop]] from elsewhere has always
had.

<<edit the active session>>=
<<reject history-only options without a hash>>
try:
    new_labels, removed, warnings, start_time = edit_active_batch(
        who=who, at=at, labels=label,
        add=add_label, remove=remove_label,
        description=description,
        containing=containing, discard=discard,
    )
except ValueError as exc:
    typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
for warning in warnings:
    typer.echo(f"Warning: {warning}", err=True)
if new_labels:
    typer.echo(
        f"Updated active session: {get_labels_display(new_labels)}"
    )
    if at is not None:
        typer.echo(
            f"New start time: {start_time:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M}"
        )
else:
    typer.echo(
        f"Discarded active session: {get_labels_display(removed)}"
    )
@

The rejections name the option that cannot work and what to do
instead, because \enquote{unknown error} on a mode the user may not
know exists would be a dead end:

<<reject history-only options without a hash>>=
if end_at is not None or duration is not None:
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --end-at and --duration require an entry hash; "
        "the active session has no end time yet, stop it first",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if (at is None and label is None and description is None
        and not add_label and not remove_label
        and not discard):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: nothing to edit for the active session; "
        "give --at, --description, --discard, -l, -a or -r",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

[[edit_active_batch]] is the session-management wrapper, shaped like
[[add_session_notes]]: everything happens in a callback under
[[mutate_worker_tracking_state]], because auto-suspend hooks make
concurrent writers ordinary and a snapshot taken outside the locks
could overwrite their work.

Parsing [[--at]] must also happen \emph{inside} the critical section.
Its reference time is the batch's current start---so that
[[--at 09:30]] resolves on the day the session began, exactly as
[[parse_edit_tracking_start_time]] anchors on the original entry in
the historical mode---and that start is only known once the session is
loaded under the lock.

<<session management>>=
def edit_active_batch(
    *,
    who: str = None,
    at: str | None = None,
    labels: list[str] | None = None,
    add: list[str] | None = None,
    remove: list[str] | None = None,
    description: str | None = None,
    containing: str | None = None,
    discard: bool = False,
) -> tuple[list[str], list[str], list[str], datetime.datetime]:
    """Edit one batch of `who`'s active session.

    The target is the batch containing the label `containing`, or
    the most recent batch when `containing` is `None`.  `discard`
    permits an edit that removes every label of the batch; the
    batch is then dropped without recording a history entry.

    Returns the batch's new labels, the removed labels, any
    warnings, and the batch's (possibly updated) start time.
    """
    resolved_who = (
        who if who is not None
        else default_username
    )
    outcome = {}

    def update(session, history_entries):
        <<resolve the target batch>>
        <<compute the session's new labels>>
        <<compute the session's new start time>>
        <<warn about discarded notes>>
        <<apply the edit or discard the batch>>
        outcome["added"] = added
        outcome["removed"] = removed
        outcome["labels"] = [
            l for l in session.batches.get(batch_id, [])
            if l in session.active_labels
        ]
        outcome["warnings"] = warnings
        outcome["start"] = new_start
        return session, history_entries

    def sync_tmux_context(session, history_entries):
        <<rewrite the current tmux context>>

    mutate_worker_tracking_state(
        update, who,
        post_update=sync_tmux_context,
    )
    return (
        outcome["labels"],
        outcome["removed"],
        outcome["warnings"],
        outcome["start"],
    )
@

Registration must come \emph{before} unregistration.  Removing a pane
file's last label deletes the file, and registration only touches
panes whose file already exists---so a pure rename processed in the
other order would delete the pane file instead of renaming its
content, silently unhooking the pane from tracking:

<<rewrite the current tmux context>>=
if outcome["added"]:
    _register_tmux_context_labels_unlocked(
        outcome["added"], resolved_who,
        session=session,
    )
if outcome["removed"]:
    _unregister_tmux_context_labels_unlocked(
        outcome["removed"], resolved_who,
    )
@

The two selectors from \cref{sec:track-edit-active} meet here:
[[--containing]] resolves through [[batch_labels_containing]], the
default through [[most_recent_batch_labels]] --- restricted, inside a
tracked window, to that window's batches
(\cref{track:window-batch-scope}), the same restriction a bare
[[stop]] applies, so the invariant that [[edit]] corrects exactly what
a following [[stop]] would end survives the scoping.  [[--containing]]
deliberately ignores the window scope: it is an explicit selector, and
its callers (scripted correction from outside any window) exist
precisely because implicit selection cannot be trusted there.  Batch
members share their start time and batch id by the [[start_labels]]
invariant, so reading them off the first member is enough:

<<resolve the target batch>>=
if containing is not None:
    batch = session.batch_labels_containing(containing)
    if not batch:
        raise ValueError(
            f"Label '{containing}' is not currently tracking"
        )
else:
    scope = current_window_batch_scope(
        session, resolved_who,
    )
    batch = session.most_recent_batch_labels(
        among=scope,
    )
    if not batch:
        if scope is not None:
            raise ValueError(
                "No active tracking session "
                "in this window"
            )
        raise ValueError("No active tracking session")
start_time, _, batch_id = session.active_labels[batch[0]]
@

The label merge reuses [[apply_label_edits]], the same rule the
historical mode and [[todo edit]] follow: [[-l]] replaces wholesale,
[[-a]]/[[-r]] merge incrementally, an absent removal or present
addition warns instead of failing, and the same label in both lists is
a contradiction that raises:

A bare [[discard]] with no label flags means \enquote{drop this whole
batch}---the empty target set expresses that directly, and the
discard branch below acts on it:

<<compute the session's new labels>>=
warnings = []
if labels is not None:
    new_set = list(labels)
elif add or remove:
    new_set, warnings = apply_label_edits(
        batch, add or [], remove or [],
    )
elif discard:
    new_set = []
else:
    new_set = batch
@

<<compute the session's new start time>>=
new_start = start_time
if at is not None:
    new_start = parse_at_time(at, reference_time=start_time)
@

Notes on a removed label are its pending description
(\cref{sec:track-edit-active} explains the mapping), and
[[edit_batch]] drops them with the label.  Losing them silently would
be a silent data loss the user cannot undo, so we warn---unless
[[--description]] is given, in which case the user is already
supplying the replacement:

<<warn about discarded notes>>=
if description is None:
    for removed_label in batch:
        if removed_label in new_set:
            continue
        if session.active_labels[removed_label][1]:
            warnings.append(
                f"notes on removed label '{removed_label}' "
                "discarded; pass --description to set the "
                "session description"
            )
@

An empty result forks three ways.  Without [[discard]] it is refused,
and the refusal is raised \emph{here} rather than left to
[[edit_batch]]'s generic guard so the message can name the flag that
confirms the intent.  With [[discard]], the batch goes through
[[discard_labels]]---the same primitive [[start --replace]] uses---so
no history entry is written: the labels were never right, and
recording time under them is exactly what the caller is avoiding.
[[discard_labels]] also cleans the batch record, and the tmux sync
needs no special case since it acts on the [[removed]] list either
way:

<<apply the edit or discard the batch>>=
if not new_set:
    if not discard:
        raise ValueError(
            "Removing every label discards the session; "
            "pass --discard to confirm (or use 'stop' "
            "to record it)"
        )
    removed = session.discard_labels(batch)
    added = []
else:
    added, removed = session.edit_batch(
        batch_id, new_set,
        start_time=new_start, notes=description,
    )
@

Let's verify the core scenario first: correcting a wrong label keeps
the session's start time and batch identity, records nothing in
history, and leaves the [[switch]] toggle alone:

<<test functions>>=
def test_edit_no_hash_swaps_wrong_label(temp_tracking_dir):
  """A hashless edit corrects labels in place."""
  runner.invoke(cli, ["start", "keep", "wrong"])
  before = load_active_session()
  start, _, batch_id = before.active_labels["keep"]

  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["edit", "-r", "wrong", "-a", "right"])

  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert "Updated active session" in result.stdout
  session = load_active_session()
  assert set(session.get_active_labels()) == {"keep", "right"}
  assert session.active_labels["right"] == (start, "", batch_id)
  assert load_tracking_data() == []

def test_edit_no_hash_keeps_switch_toggle(temp_tracking_dir):
  """Correcting labels leaves the switch toggle untouched."""
  runner.invoke(cli, ["start", "A"])
  runner.invoke(cli, ["switch", "B"])

  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["edit", "-a", "C"])

  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert load_active_session().switch_other == ["A"]
@

Replacement and start-time correction exercise the other options.  The
new start must resolve against the day the session began, and a bare
[[stop]] afterwards must end the corrected batch:

<<test functions>>=
def test_edit_no_hash_replaces_labels(temp_tracking_dir):
  """`-l` replaces the whole label set of the running batch."""
