\subsection{Today Command}
\label{sec:track-today}

The [[today]] command answers the most immediate version of the
question teachers ask constantly---\enquote{how much time did I
actually spend on this?}---for the one day where the answer can still
change what happens next: \enquote{what does today look like, and am I
ahead or behind?}  It names one calendar date, today, and nothing
else.  Longer horizons are the business of the period reports in
\cref{sec:track-stats}; a window to configure here would only make
this a worse spelling of [[stats daily]].

The output is aggregated, but not flattened into individual labels.
Teachers often work in repeated bursts on the same combination of
tags, such as [[nytid]], [[devel]], and [[work]].  Listing each
session separately is noisy, but listing each label individually
falsely suggests a hierarchy.  Grouping by the exact label set keeps
the tags equal while still collapsing repeated work into one line.

The day is compared with what the work week
(\cref{cli.utils.workweek}) expects of it: the daily limit on a work
day, nothing on a day off.  This replaces the prorated weekly warning
the old summary printed, which had to explain that a three-day window
warned after \(40 \cdot 3/7\) hours.  A single day needs no
proration; the hours are owed or they are not, and an hour tracked on
a Saturday shows as a deviation just as an hour short on a Monday
does.  The expectation and the deviation sit in the table rather than
in a line printed around it, because \cref{cli.utils.output} promises
that a piped listing is rows and nothing else.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
today_labels_arg = typer.Argument(
    help="Filter today's statistics by specific labels "
         "(shows all if not specified)",
    autocompletion=complete_historical_labels)
daily_limit_opt = typer.Option(
    "--daily-limit",
    help="Hours expected per work day "
         "(uses config default if not specified). "
         "[config: track.daily_limit]")
@

<<today command>>=
@cli.command()
def today(
    labels: Annotated[list[str],
                      today_labels_arg] = None,
    daily_limit: Annotated[float | None,
                           daily_limit_opt] = None,
    who: Annotated[str | None,
                   who_filter_opt] = None,
):
    """
    Show today's tracked time against the day's expectation.

    Label sets are grouped, largest first, under the day's total, the
    hours expected of it and the difference between the two.

    \b
    Examples:
      nytid track today
      nytid track today DD1310 lecture
      nytid track today --who Dan-Claude
    """
    <<resolve today's expectation>>
    <<load and clip today's entries>>
    <<build today's table rows>>
    <<print today's listing>>
@

The [[--daily-limit]] flag is optional because users should be able to
configure the expectation once and forget about it; without either,
the module falls back to the built-in eight hours.  The work-day mask
is read once here and handed down to every helper, the way
[[schedule]] does it, so that a test controls the calendar by patching
one name in this module rather than the configuration.

<<resolve today's expectation>>=
if daily_limit is None:
    daily_limit = get_default_daily_limit()
work_days = get_work_days()
@

The shared helper handles the mechanical filtering by worker and
labels, and distinguishes \enquote{no data at all} ([[None]]) from
\enquote{data exists but nothing matched}.  An empty match is not an
early return, because an empty day is exactly what this command must
report.

Clipping to the calendar date rather than the last 24 hours is the
argument of \cref{sec:track-calendar-periods}.  We capture one
timestamp so that the clip and the printed date agree even if
midnight passes between the two.

<<load and clip today's entries>>=
now = datetime.datetime.now()
today_date = now.date()
entries = filter_entries(who=who, labels=labels)

if entries is None:
    typer.echo("No tracking data available")
    return

entries = clip_entries_to_dates(entries, today_date, today_date)
daily_totals = compute_daily_wall_clock(entries)
daily_label_set_totals = compute_daily_label_set_totals(entries)
@

The report is read comparatively: down the last column to compare
label sets, across the first row to connect the day with its total.
That is a table, and since \cref{cli.utils.output} the project has
exactly one piece of code that knows how to print one, so all this
command has left to do is produce rows and name its columns.

The first row carries the day---date, total, expectation and
difference---together with the largest label set; the other label
sets follow on continuation rows whose leading fields are empty.
Building the rows before printing them is what stops the two
audiences from receiving two different reports: the continuation
layout is decided here, not inside a renderer, so a pipe sees the
same grouping a terminal does.

A day without matching entries still gets a row.  [[print_rows]]
prints nothing for no rows, which is the honest answer for most
listings but would hide the loudest thing this command can say: eight
hours owed and none tracked.

<<build today's table rows>>=
label_totals = daily_label_set_totals.get(today_date, {})
duration = daily_totals.get(today_date, datetime.timedelta())
expected = expected_time(
    today_date, today_date, daily_limit, work_days
)
day_fields = (
    today_date.isoformat(),
    format_duration(duration),
    format_duration(expected, seconds=False),
    format_signed_duration(duration - expected, seconds=False),
)
continuation = ("", "", "", "")

rows = []
for labels_key, labels_duration in sorted(
    label_totals.items(),
    key=lambda item: (-item[1].total_seconds(), item[0]),
):
    head = day_fields if not rows else continuation
    rows.append(
        head
        + (", ".join(labels_key), format_duration(labels_duration))
    )
if not rows:
    rows.append(day_fields + ("(off)", "0s"))
@

Only [[Labels]] folds, because it is the only cell whose width the
data controls: a label set can be arbitrarily long, while dates and
formatted durations are as wide as their formatter makes them and no
wider.

<<print today's listing>>=
print_rows(
    [
        Column("Date", style="cyan", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Total", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Expected", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Diff", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Labels", fold=True),
        Column("Time", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
    ],
    rows,
)
@

The piped form is six tab-separated fields per row and nothing else.
It used to be an \textsc{ascii} table computed in the command, with a
header, a rule of dashes, and cells padded to the widest value in the
column---which made the byte offset of every field depend on the
data, so [[cut -c]] worked for one day and failed the next, and
[[cut -f]] never worked at all.  Continuation rows are what let
[[cut -f5,6]] read every label line without working out where a day
begins: they are rows with empty leading fields, not short rows.  The
argument in \cref{sec:track-piped-listings} for why changing these
bytes is safe covers this command as well: the same
[[nytid.hourly.sh]] that never invokes [[track ls]] never invokes
[[track today]] or [[track stats]] either.

Let us pin the bytes, taking a day with two label sets so that a
continuation row is part of what is pinned.  Every day is a work day
in these tests, so that they pass on a Saturday too.

<<test functions>>=
def test_today_pipes_headerless_tsv(temp_tracking_dir, monkeypatch):
  """Piped output is bare TSV rows, continuations included."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "nytid.cli.track.get_work_days", lambda: DAY_NAMES
  )
  today = datetime.datetime.now().date().isoformat()
  runner.invoke(cli, [
    "add", "DD1310",
    "--duration", "1",
    "--at", f"{today} 09:00"
  ])
  runner.invoke(cli, [
    "add", "DD1321",
    "--duration", "30m",
    "--at", f"{today} 11:00"
  ])

  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["today"])

  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert result.stdout == (
    f"{today}\t1h 30m 0s\t8h 0m\t-6h 30m\tDD1310\t1h 0m 0s\n"
    "\t\t\t\tDD1321\t30m 0s\n"
  )
@

An empty work day is owed in full, and a day off expects nothing, so
any time tracked on it is a positive deviation:

<<test functions>>=
def test_today_shows_an_empty_work_day_as_owed(
    temp_tracking_dir, monkeypatch
):
  """An empty work day is a row, not silence."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    "nytid.cli.track.get_work_days", lambda: DAY_NAMES
  )
  today = datetime.datetime.now().date()
  yesterday = today - datetime.timedelta(days=1)
  runner.invoke(cli, [
    "add", "work", "--duration", "1",
    "--at", f"{yesterday.isoformat()} 10:00"
  ])

  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["today"])

  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert result.stdout == (
    f"{today.isoformat()}\t0s\t8h 0m\t-8h 0m\t(off)\t0s\n"
  )

def test_today_expects_nothing_on_a_day_off(
    temp_tracking_dir, monkeypatch
):
  """Time tracked on a day off is all deviation."""
  monkeypatch.setattr("nytid.cli.track.get_work_days", lambda: [])
  today = datetime.datetime.now().date().isoformat()
  runner.invoke(cli, [
    "add", "work", "--duration", "1", "--at", f"{today} 10:00"
  ])

  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["today"])

  assert result.exit_code == 0
  assert f"{today}\t1h 0m 0s\t0m\t+1h 0m\twork\t1h 0m 0s\n" == (
    result.stdout
  )
@

\subsection{The \texttt{stats} Command Group}
\label{sec:track-stats}

[[today]] answers for one day.  The [[stats]] group answers the
question the user actually asks over a term: \enquote{we are supposed
to work eight hours a weekday and nothing at weekends---how far off
are we?}  It has four subcommands, [[daily]], [[weekly]], [[monthly]]
and [[yearly]], and each prints one row per period, newest first, with
the time tracked, the time the work week expected, and the difference.
A summary of how the work was done follows the table.

The four are one report at four zoom levels.  The period helpers of
\cref{sec:track-calendar-periods} already made the granularity a
value, so the subcommands are four twelve-line wrappers around one
function, and the only thing a reader has to compare between them is
the default number of periods.

A bare [[nytid track stats]] prints the group's help.  Typer's default
for a group without a command is a usage error, and this is the most
typed command in the module; an error there would be a trap set for
every user who remembers the old name.

<<stats group>>=
stats = typer.Typer(
    name="stats",
    no_args_is_help=True,
    help=(
        "Tracked time per period, compared with the work week.\n\n"
        "One row per day, week, month or year, newest first, with "
        "the hours expected of it and the difference. With labels, "
        "the labels' share of all tracked time instead. A summary "
        "of how the work was done follows on a terminal.\n\n"
        "Use \"nytid track today\" for today's label sets.\n\n"
        "Examples:\n\n\b"
        "  nytid track stats daily -n 14\n"
        "  nytid track stats weekly DD1310\n"
        "  nytid track stats monthly -n 12 --no-summary | cut -f1,4\n"
    ))
cli.add_typer(stats)
@

\subsubsection{Options}

The summary is what makes the report interesting, so it should not
have to be asked for; but \cref{cli.utils.output} promises that a
piped listing is rows and nothing else, and [[cut -f4]] on a report
with a paragraph after it is exactly the breakage that promise
exists to prevent.  The flag is therefore three-valued.  Unset means
\emph{auto}: the summary is printed when standard output is a
terminal and omitted when it is a pipe.  [[--summary]] and
[[--no-summary]] force it either way, and the configuration key can
do the same permanently.  [[default]] returns the fallback untouched,
so an unset key yields [[None]] and thereby auto.

<<constants>>=
TRACK_STATS_SUMMARY_CONFIG = "track.stats.summary"
DEFAULT_TRACK_STATS_SUMMARY = default(
    TRACK_STATS_SUMMARY_CONFIG, None, bool
)
@

The number of periods has a default per granularity, chosen so that
the default report fits on a screen and answers a natural question:
the past week by day, the past month by week, the past half year by
month, and the past few years.  These are plain constants rather than
configuration keys; [[-n]] is short enough to type.

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_STATS_DAILY_COUNT = 7
DEFAULT_STATS_WEEKLY_COUNT = 4
DEFAULT_STATS_MONTHLY_COUNT = 6
DEFAULT_STATS_YEARLY_COUNT = 3
@

<<argument and option definitions>>=
stats_labels_arg = typer.Argument(
    help="Filter the statistics by specific labels "
         "(shows the labels' share of all tracked time)",
    autocompletion=complete_historical_labels)
stats_count_opt = typer.Option(
    "--count", "-n",
    help="Number of periods to include, newest first, "
         "counting the current one")
summary_opt = typer.Option(
    "--summary/--no-summary",
    help="Print the work summary after the table "
         "(by default only on a terminal). "
         "[config: track.stats.summary]")
@

