\subsubsection{The Period Report}

The report is a function rather than a shared chunk because it is
reached from four code paths, and a function makes its inputs
explicit where a chunk would leave them to whichever command happened
to define the right variables (\cref{sec:track-code-outline}).

<<period report>>=
def run_period_stats(
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
    count: int,
    labels: list[str] | None,
    who: str | None,
    daily_limit: float | None,
    summary: bool | None,
):
    """Print the period report shared by the stats subcommands."""
    <<reject a count below one period>>
    <<resolve the period report's settings>>
    <<load and clip entries for the period report>>
    <<compute the per-period totals>>
    <<build the period table rows>>
    <<print the period listing>>
    <<print the work summary>>
@

A report needs at least one period to describe.  Rejecting the input
is better than clamping it, because a clamp quietly reports something
the user did not ask for; the message names the flag, since there is
no configuration key that could have supplied the value.

<<reject a count below one period>>=
if count < 1:
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: -n must be at least 1, got {count}",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

The settings are resolved the same way [[today]] resolves them, with
the summary's auto value decided here, once, from the stream the
rows will go to.

<<resolve the period report's settings>>=
if daily_limit is None:
    daily_limit = get_default_daily_limit()
if summary is None:
    summary = sys.stdout.isatty()
work_days = get_work_days()
@

The window is the union of the periods, oldest start to newest end,
and the entries are clipped to it once.  The reporting end is the
window clipped to today, for the expectation; the newest period is
never over.

With labels the report shows a share, and a share needs the whole:
the same window loaded again without the label filter.  This is a
second read of one JSON file, which is the honest cost of the
column.  Filtering the unfiltered list in memory instead would save
the read but tie the two loads together in a way that a later change
to [[filter_entries]] could silently break, and the read is cheap.

<<load and clip entries for the period report>>=
now = datetime.datetime.now()
today_date = now.date()
periods = make_period_ranges(granularity, count, now=now)
if not periods:
    return
window_start = periods[-1].start
window_end = periods[0].end
reporting_end = min(window_end, today_date)

entries = filter_entries(who=who, labels=labels)
if entries is None:
    typer.echo("No tracking data available")
    return
entries = clip_entries_to_dates(entries, window_start, window_end)

all_entries = None
if labels:
    all_entries = clip_entries_to_dates(
        filter_entries(who=who), window_start, window_end
    )
@

The totals follow the pipeline of \cref{sec:track-calendar-periods}.
The weekly, monthly and yearly rows also say on how many of the
period's work days anything was tracked, which needs the same
bucketing applied to a count of dates rather than a sum of durations.

<<compute the per-period totals>>=
daily_totals = compute_daily_wall_clock(entries)
period_totals = totals_per_period(daily_totals, granularity)
worked_per_period = worked_days_per_period(
    daily_totals, granularity, work_days
)
all_totals = None
if all_entries is not None:
    all_totals = totals_per_period(
        compute_daily_wall_clock(all_entries), granularity
    )
@

<<helper functions>>=
def worked_days_per_period(
    daily_totals: dict[datetime.date, datetime.timedelta],
    granularity: StatsPeriod,
    work_days: list[str],
) -> dict[datetime.date, int]:
    """Count work days with tracked time per period start."""
    counts = {}
    for date, duration in daily_totals.items():
        if duration <= datetime.timedelta():
            continue
        if not is_work_day(date, work_days):
            continue
        start, _ = period_bounds(granularity, date)
        counts[start] = counts.get(start, 0) + 1
    return counts
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_worked_days_per_period_counts_work_days_only():
  """Only work days with time are counted, per period."""
  weekdays = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"]
  daily_totals = {
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7): datetime.timedelta(hours=8),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 8): datetime.timedelta(),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 12): datetime.timedelta(hours=2),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 14): datetime.timedelta(hours=1),
  }
  assert worked_days_per_period(
    daily_totals, StatsPeriod.weekly, weekdays
  ) == {
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7): 1,
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 14): 1,
  }
@

Two shapes of row, decided by whether labels were given.  Without
labels a period is compared with its expectation; with labels the
expectation is meaningless---nobody owes eight hours a day to one
course---so the comparison is with everything tracked in the period
instead.  The daily rows leave out the work-day count and the
average, which for a single day would only restate the other columns.

<<build the period table rows>>=
rows = []
for period in periods:
    tracked = period_totals.get(period.start, datetime.timedelta())
    if labels:
        <<append a share row for [[period]]>>
    else:
        <<append an expectation row for [[period]]>>
@

The expectation of a period stops at today, so the current week is
compared with the work days that have passed.  The average is per
work day in that same clipped range, so a full week and a week in
progress are comparable.  A period without work days---a weekend
seen daily, say---has no average, and a dash is more honest than a
zero.

<<append an expectation row for [[period]]>>=
period_end = min(period.end, today_date)
expected = expected_time(
    period.start, period_end, daily_limit, work_days
)
row = (
    period.label,
    format_duration(tracked, seconds=False),
    format_duration(expected, seconds=False),
    format_signed_duration(tracked - expected, seconds=False),
)
if granularity != StatsPeriod.daily:
    workdays = count_work_days(period.start, period_end, work_days)
    worked = worked_per_period.get(period.start, 0)
    average = "-"
    if workdays:
        average = format_duration(tracked / workdays, seconds=False)
    row += (f"{worked}/{workdays}", average)
rows.append(row)
@

<<append a share row for [[period]]>>=
all_tracked = all_totals.get(period.start, datetime.timedelta())
share = "-"
if all_tracked:
    share = f"{round(100 * tracked / all_tracked)}%"
rows.append((
    period.label,
    format_duration(tracked, seconds=False),
    format_duration(all_tracked, seconds=False),
    share,
))
@

The header is [[Period]] whatever the granularity, so that one
implementation keeps one vocabulary and a reader of the piped form
can count on column four meaning the same thing in every report.

<<print the period listing>>=
if labels:
    columns = [
        Column("Period", style="cyan", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Tracked", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("All", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Share", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
    ]
else:
    columns = [
        Column("Period", style="cyan", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Tracked", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Expected", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        Column("Diff", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
    ]
    if granularity != StatsPeriod.daily:
        columns += [
            Column("Days", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
            Column("Avg", justify="right", no_wrap=True),
        ]
print_rows(columns, rows)
@

The summary covers the whole window at once, computed by
\cref{sec:track-work-summary} from the same clipped entries, and is
separated from the table by a blank line.

<<print the work summary>>=
if not summary:
    return
work_summary = compute_work_summary(
    entries, window_start, reporting_end, daily_limit, work_days
)
all_total = None
if all_totals is not None:
    all_total = sum(all_totals.values(), datetime.timedelta())
typer.echo()
for line in work_summary_lines(
    work_summary, periods, labels, all_total
):
    typer.echo(line)
@

\subsubsection{Rendering the Summary}

The summary is six lines at most, each dropped when it has nothing to
say: a window with no work has no longest day, and a report without a
day off with work should not announce that there were none.  The
lines are plain \textsc{ascii} so that they survive every terminal
and every log file.

<<helper functions>>=
def work_summary_lines(
    summary: WorkSummary,
    periods: list[Period],
    labels: list[str] | None = None,
    all_total: datetime.timedelta | None = None,
) -> list[str]:
    """Render a `WorkSummary` as the lines the period reports print.

    With `labels`, the headline compares the labels' time with
    `all_total`, the time tracked on everything in the window.
    """
    lines = []
    <<the headline>>
    <<the work days line>>
    <<the days off line>>
    <<the extremes line>>
    <<the typical day line>>
    <<the top label sets line>>
    return lines
@

The headline names the window by its periods and its length in days,
then gives the one number the report exists for: tracked against
expected, or, with labels, tracked against everything.

<<the headline>>=
span = periods[0].label
if len(periods) > 1:
    span = f"{periods[-1].label} to {periods[0].label}"
days = (summary.end_date - summary.start_date).days + 1
tracked = format_duration(summary.total, seconds=False)
if labels:
    share = "-"
    if all_total:
        share = f"{round(100 * summary.total / all_total)}%"
    lines.append(
        f"{span}, {count_noun(days, 'day')}, {', '.join(labels)}: "
        f"tracked {tracked} of "
        f"{format_duration(all_total, seconds=False)} in total "
        f"({share})"
    )
else:
    lines.append(
        f"{span}, {count_noun(days, 'day')}: tracked {tracked} of "
        f"{format_duration(summary.expected, seconds=False)} "
        f"expected "
        f"({format_signed_duration(summary.diff, seconds=False)})"
    )
@

<<the work days line>>=
line = (
    f"worked {summary.days_worked} of "
    f"{count_noun(summary.workdays, 'work day')}"
)
if labels:
    line = (
        f"worked on these labels {summary.days_worked} of "
        f"{count_noun(summary.workdays, 'work day')}"
    )
if summary.per_workday is not None:
    line += (
        f", {format_duration(summary.per_workday, seconds=False)}"
        " per work day"
    )
lines.append(line)
@

<<the days off line>>=
if summary.off_days_worked:
    lines.append(
        f"{count_noun(summary.off_days_worked, 'day')} off with "
        f"work, {format_duration(summary.off_days_total, seconds=False)}"
        " in total"
    )
@

<<the extremes line>>=
if summary.longest_day is not None:
    date, duration = summary.longest_day
    lines.append(
        f"longest day {date.isoformat()}, "
        f"{format_duration(duration, seconds=False)}; "
        f"longest streak "
        f"{count_noun(summary.longest_streak, 'day')}"
    )
@

<<the typical day line>>=
if summary.median_start is not None:
    lines.append(
        f"typical day {summary.median_start:%H:%M} to "
        f"{summary.median_end:%H:%M}"
    )
@

<<the top label sets line>>=
if summary.top_label_sets and summary.total:
    parts = [
        f"{', '.join(labels_key)} "
        f"{round(100 * duration / summary.total)}%"
        for labels_key, duration in summary.top_label_sets
    ]
    lines.append("top label sets: " + "; ".join(parts))
@

English plurals are the one formatting rule the lines share.

<<helper functions>>=
def count_noun(count: int, noun: str) -> str:
    """Return `count` with `noun`, pluralised unless `count` is 1."""
    return f"{count} {noun}" if count == 1 else f"{count} {noun}s"
@

Let's verify the rendering against the fixed week of
\cref{sec:track-work-summary}, without and with a label filter, and
that an empty window renders only the lines that apply:

<<test functions>>=
def summary_week_periods():
  return [Period(
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7), datetime.date(2026, 9, 13), "2026-W37"
  )]

def test_work_summary_lines_for_a_week():
  """Every line of the summary, in order."""
  weekdays = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"]
  summary = compute_work_summary(
    summary_week_entries(),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 13),
    8.0,
    weekdays,
  )
  assert work_summary_lines(summary, summary_week_periods()) == [
    "2026-W37, 7 days: tracked 27h 0m of 40h 0m expected (-13h 0m)",
    "worked 3 of 5 work days, 5h 24m per work day",
    "1 day off with work, 2h 0m in total",
    "longest day 2026-09-08, 10h 30m; longest streak 3 days",
    "typical day 09:15 to 16:30",
    "top label sets: nytid 69%; DD1310 24%; email 7%",
  ]

def test_work_summary_lines_with_labels():
  """With labels the headline is a share of everything tracked."""
  weekdays = ["mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri"]
  summary = compute_work_summary(
    summary_week_entries(),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 7),
    datetime.date(2026, 9, 13),
